  "text": "\n\n \n \n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n Remote Code Execution in Elo Ruby backend via unescaped `#{...}` interpolation in emitted string literals and subtype-constraint labels · Advisory · enspirit/elo · GitHub \n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n\n\n \n\n\n \n\n\n \n \n\n \n \n\n \n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n\n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n \n \n \n \n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n \n \n\n\n \n\n\n \n\n \n \n\n \n \n \n\n \n \n\n\n \n\n \n\n \n \n \n \n\n \n Skip to content \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n Navigation Menu Sign in Products Platform GitHub Repository hosting, code review, and collaboration Copilot AI coding agent for tasks, projects and automated work Build & Ship Actions CI/CD and automation Code Quality Quality analysis, fixes, and enforcement Codespaces Cloud development environments Secure Code Security Vulnerability detection and remediation Secret Protection Secret scanning and leak prevention All Features Developers Learn Documentation Training Certifications Build Build with Copilot Customize Copilot Integrations Explore Trending repos GitHub Sponsors Developer articles Changelog Changelog Your source for the latest features, improvements, and fixes across GitHub products, all in one place. Businesses By Stage Enterprises Team Startups Nonprofits By Industry Healthcare Financial services Manufacturing Government See all industries Evaluate GitHub Why GitHub Customer Stories Business insights Trust Center Add-ons GitHub Advanced Security Enterprise-grade code security Copilot for business Enterprise-grade AI features Premium Support Enterprise-grade 24/7 support Resources Learn & Connect Documentation Resource center Blog Events & webinars Community Forum Customer Stories Integrations Support Customer support Training GitHub Status Company About Partners Changelog GitHub Universe GitHub Universe The event for developers, by developers — talks, workshops, and what’s next for GitHub. Pricing Search / Sign in Sign up Appearance settings \n \n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n You signed in with another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. \n You signed out in another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. \n You switched accounts on another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. \n\n \n Dismiss alert \n\n\n \n \n \n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n {{ message }} \n\n \n \n \n \n \n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n enspirit elo Repository navigation Code Issues Pull requests 3  ( 3 ) Actions Projects Security and quality 3  ( 3 ) Insights More items \n \n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n \n \n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n elo \n Security \n Advisories \n GHSA-7w38-ggh5-v542 \n \n \n\n \n\n \n \n\n \n Remote Code Execution in Elo Ruby backend via unescaped `#{...}` interpolation in emitted string literals and subtype-constraint labels\n \n\n \n\n \n\n\n \n \n Critical\n \n \n\n \n blambeau \n published\n GHSA-7w38-ggh5-v542 \n Oct 30, 2026 \n\n \n \n \n \n\n\n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Package \n\n \n \n \n\n \n @enspirit/elo \n ( npm )\n \n \n \n\n \n Affected versions \n\n <= 1.0.0 \n \n\n \n Patched versions \n\n >= 1.0.1 \n \n \n\n \n \n\n\n \n \n Description \n \n\n \n \n Summary \n Elo's Ruby backend emits attacker-controlled text into Ruby double-quoted strings without escaping the # character. Because # begins Ruby's interpolation sequences — #{expr} , #@ivar , #@@cvar , #$gvar — and any expression wrapped in #{...} may use backticks for shell execution, an Elo program that contains a crafted string literal or a labelled subtype constraint compiles to Ruby that evaluates attacker-chosen Ruby, and through backticks runs attacker-chosen shell commands, at runtime. \n The same Elo source compiled to JavaScript, Python, or SQL is benign, so the bug also breaks the cross-target portability guarantee the rest of the compiler honours. \n Vulnerable sinks \n Two sinks exist in src/compilers/ruby.ts at commit 749af7b : \n \n \n String-literal emitter — lines 180–184. \nThe emitter escapes backslash, double quote, and control characters, but not # . Any #{...} sequence in an Elo string literal flows straight into the emitted Ruby source. \n \n \n Labelled subtype-constraint branch — lines 405–412. \nThe error message passed to p_fail(p, \"...\") is wrapped in a Ruby double-quoted string with no escape pass at all. Because the parser at src/parser.ts:1339–1348 accepts single-quoted Elo strings as constraint labels, a label such as '#{ `id` }' flows unchanged into the emitted Ruby. \n \n \n Attack surfaces \n All three surfaces share the same root cause (the missing # escape in the Ruby double-quoted-string emission path) and are addressed by the same patch: \n \n \n \n # \n Entry point \n Threat model \n \n \n \n \n 1 \n /try playground on elo-lang.org (Ruby target) \n 0-click server-side RCE against elo-lang.org infrastructure from a shared PoC URL \n \n \n 2 \n bin/eloc -t ruby -x on a CI or DevOps host \n RCE on any host that passes Elo strings through the CLI \n \n \n 3 \n Library API — defaultFormats.elo.parse() with a Ruby emission step \n Ruby RCE for any downstream integrator using Elo as a rule engine, configuration DSL, or form builder DSL (the primary documented use cases) \n \n \n \n Proof of Concept \n String-literal sink: \n # poc.elo\n'RES=#{`id 2>&1`}'\n \n $ eloc -t ruby -x poc.elo\n# emitted Ruby contains: \"RES=#{`id 2>&1`}\"\n# running it returns the output of id(1).\n \n Replacing `id 2>&1` with `touch /tmp/pwned` produces a trivially observable filesystem side effect. \n Subtype-constraint label sink: \n # poc-subtype.elo\nlet X = Int(i | '#{`touch /tmp/pwned2`}': i > 0) in X(-1)\n \n The predicate fails for -1 , causing p_fail(p, \"#{...}\") to interpolate and execute the backtick command at the Ruby layer. \n Both flows are automated in the reproduction script submitted with the original report and were verified against Ruby 3.x. \n Root cause \n The Ruby-emitter's double-quoted-string escape routine omitted # from its character set, and one emission path (the subtype-constraint label branch) performed no escape pass at all. \n \n \n\n \n\n \n \n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n \n \n Severity \n \n \n Critical\n \n\n \n \n \n 9.8 \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n CVSS overall score\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n This score calculates overall vulnerability severity from 0 to 10 and is based on the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS). \n \n \n \n \n / 10 \n \n \n CVSS v3 base metrics \n\n \n Attack vector\n Network \n \n Attack complexity\n Low \n \n Privileges required\n None \n \n User interaction\n None \n \n Scope\n Unchanged \n \n Confidentiality\n High \n \n Integrity\n High \n \n Availability\n High \n \n \n \n Learn more about base metrics \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n CVSS v3 base metrics\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Attack vector: \n More severe the more the remote (logically and physically) an attacker can be in order to exploit the vulnerability. \n \n \n Attack complexity: \n More severe for the least complex attacks. \n \n \n Privileges required: \n More severe if no privileges are required. \n \n \n User interaction: \n More severe when no user interaction is required. \n \n \n Scope: \n More severe when a scope change occurs, e.g. one vulnerable component impacts resources in components beyond its security scope. \n \n \n Confidentiality: \n More severe when loss of data confidentiality is highest, measuring the level of data access available to an unauthorized user. \n \n \n Integrity: \n More severe when loss of data integrity is the highest, measuring the consequence of data modification possible by an unauthorized user. \n \n \n Availability: \n More severe when the loss of impacted component availability is highest. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H \n\n \n\n \n \n CVE ID \n \n CVE-2026-44266\n \n \n\n \n Weaknesses \n \n \n Weakness \n CWE-94 \n \n \n \n Improper Control of Generation of Code \n The product constructs all or part of a code segment using externally-influenced input from an upstream component, but it does not neutralize or incorrectly neutralizes special elements that could modify the syntax or behavior of the intended code segment.\n Learn more on MITRE. \n\n \n \n\n \n \n Weakness \n CWE-116 \n \n \n \n Improper Encoding or Escaping of Output \n The product prepares a structured message for communication with another component, but encoding or escaping of the data is either missing or done incorrectly. As a result, the intended structure of the message is not preserved.\n Learn more on MITRE. \n\n \n \n\n \n \n Weakness \n CWE-1336 \n \n \n \n Improper Neutralization of Special Elements Used in a Template Engine \n The product uses a template engine to insert or process externally-influenced input, but it does not neutralize or incorrectly neutralizes special elements or syntax that can be interpreted as template expressions or other code directives when processed by the engine.\n Learn more on MITRE. \n\n \n \n\n \n\n \n Credits \n\n \n \n \n \n \n ultra-supara \n\n \n Reporter \n\n \n\n \n\n \n \n\n\n\n\n \n\n \n \n\n\n \n\n \n\n \n \n\n \n Footer \n\n \n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n &copy; 2026 GitHub, Inc.\n \n \n\n \n Footer navigation \n\n \n\n\n \n Terms \n \n\n \n Privacy \n \n\n\n \n Security \n \n\n \n Status \n \n\n \n Community \n \n\n \n Docs \n \n\n \n Contact \n \n\n \n \n \n \n Manage cookies\n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n Do not share my personal information\n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \