webinars Community Forum Customer Stories Integrations Support Customer support Training GitHub Status Company About Partners Changelog GitHub Universe GitHub Universe The event for developers, by developers — talks, workshops, and what’s next for GitHub. Pricing Search / Sign in Sign up Appearance settings \n \n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n You signed in with another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. \n You signed out in another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. \n You switched accounts on another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. \n\n \n Dismiss alert \n\n\n \n \n \n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n {{ message }} \n\n \n \n \n \n \n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n FreeRDP FreeRDP Repository navigation Code Issues 155  ( 155 ) Pull requests 43  ( 43 ) Discussions Actions Projects Wiki Security and quality 325  ( 325 ) Insights More items \n \n\n\n \n\n\n\n \n \n \n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n FreeRDP \n Security \n Advisories \n GHSA-jq25-3374-4262 \n \n \n\n \n\n \n \n\n \n Incomplete fix for GHSA-43hh-p3vw-hfx3: CN-fallback and IP-literal cert identity checks were never patched\n \n\n \n \n This report was submitted with AI assistance\n \n \n\n \n\n\n \n \n High\n \n \n\n \n akallabeth \n published\n GHSA-jq25-3374-4262 \n Oct 19, 2026 \n\n \n \n \n \n\n\n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Package \n\n \n FreeRDP \n (C)\n \n \n \n\n \n Affected versions \n\n <= 3.32.1 \n \n\n \n Patched versions \n\n 3.32.2 \n \n \n\n \n \n\n\n \n \n Description \n \n\n \n \n Summary \n GHSA-43hh-p3vw-hfx3 (patched_versions: 3.29.0) claims to fix three certificate identity-validation weaknesses in tls_verify_certificate() / tls_match_hostname() / x509_utils_get_dns_names() , but the actual 3.29.0 fix set (commits af7f228 , c5a7325 , b7db8d2 ) only addresses the embedded-NUL-byte truncation case (Case 1); the Common-Name-fallback-despite-non-matching-SAN case (Case 2) and the IP-literal-matched-via-DNS-SAN case (Case 3) are byte-identical in libfreerdp/crypto/tls.c from the 3.29.0 tag through current HEAD. Under a trusted-but-misissued or rogue-CA-issued certificate, a man-in-the-middle can still silently pass FreeRDP's client-side TLS identity check for Case 2 and Case 3, exactly as described in the original advisory, with no FreeRDP release since ever actually closing them. \n Details \n Component \n libfreerdp/crypto/tls.c ( tls_verify_certificate() , tls_match_hostname() ) and libfreerdp/crypto/x509_utils.c ( x509_utils_get_dns_names() , x509_utils_get_common_name() ), called from the client TLS handshake path at tls.c:1093 . \n Threat model \n Identical to GHSA-43hh-p3vw-hfx3 's own stated model: an attacker able to present a certificate that chains to a CA the FreeRDP client already trusts (a misissuing public CA, a compromised or rogue private CA, or any other certificate-trust-chain anomaly), positioned to intercept or redirect the RDP TCP connection. No code execution or credential extraction is claimed; the impact is a silent bypass of server-identity verification in that threat model, enabling full session man-in-the-middle (screen, keystrokes, clipboard, input injection). \n Root cause \n tls_verify_certificate() (current HEAD, libfreerdp/crypto/tls.c ~line 1856-1879): \n if ( common_name ) {\n if ( tls_match_hostname ( common_name , common_name_length , hostname ))\n hostname_match = TRUE;\n}\n if ( dns_names ) {\n for ( size_t index = 0 ; index < dns_names_count ; index ++ ) {\n if ( tls_match_hostname ( dns_names [ index ], dns_names_lengths [ index ], hostname )) {\n hostname_match = TRUE;\n break ;\n }\n }\n} \n This is an unconditional OR with no RFC 6125 §6.4.4 rule that presence of any dNSName SAN entry should make the Common Name irrelevant for identity purposes. A certificate with CN=<target hostname> and a SAN list containing only non-matching names still sets hostname_match = TRUE through the CN branch alone (Case 2). \n Separately, x509_utils_get_dns_names() ( x509_utils.c:667 , map_subject_alt_name(xcert, GEN_DNS, extract_string, &list) ) only ever requests GEN_DNS entries; extract_string_generic() 's switch handles GEN_URI / GEN_DNS / GEN_EMAIL and silently skips everything else ( default: return 1; ), so a true GEN_IPADD ( iPAddress ) SAN entry is never read by any code path in the project. When the RDP target is an IP literal, FreeRDP falls back to matching it as a plain string against CN/DNS-SAN entries via the same tls_match_hostname() , so a certificate whose CN or DNS-SAN happens to equal the IP-literal string (with no real iPAddress SAN at all) is accepted (Case 3). \n None of the three commits that shipped in 3.29.0 for this GHSA touch this OR-logic block or add any GEN_IPADD handling: \n \n af7f2280f only tightens wildcard ( *. ) subdomain-depth matching (an unrelated sibling advisory, GHSA-5wr6-8m8j-3h7f ). \n c5a7325f1 only restricts accepted X.509 purposes to X509_PURPOSE_SSL_SERVER (another unrelated sibling advisory, GHSA-89c6-jjrw-96h4 ). \n b7db8d222 adds check_string_is_host_or_ip() (length-aware string extraction), which genuinely fixes Case 1 only. \n \n Already-known check \n Searched FreeRDP's open/closed issues and all currently published security advisories (100 fetched via the GitHub API) for certificate/hostname/CN/SAN-fallback keywords. No open issue or advisory other than GHSA-43hh-p3vw-hfx3 itself describes this residual gap, and GHSA-43hh-p3vw-hfx3 's own vulnerabilities[].patched_versions field states 3.29.0 , which this report shows to be inaccurate for 2 of its 3 documented cases. \n Suggested fix \n Case 2: only consult the Common Name when the certificate has zero dNSName SAN entries at all (RFC 6125 §6.4.4); if any SAN entries are present, ignore CN entirely regardless of match/mismatch. Case 3: add a GEN_IPADD -aware extraction path ( x509_utils_get_ip_addresses() or similar) and, when the connection target parses as an IP literal, match exclusively against iPAddress SAN entries (binary comparison, not string formatting) — never fall back to CN/DNS-SAN string matching for IP-literal targets. Both are exactly the behaviors OpenSSL's own X509_check_host() / X509_check_ip_asc() already implement; the original advisory's own stated remediation direction (\"delegating identity validation to OpenSSL APIs\") would resolve both if actually adopted instead of hand-rolled string matching. \n AI Use Disclosure \n AI (Claude) was used as a tool under my direction for code review, build automation, and reproduction-harness development. I reviewed and verified the AI-generated results — including independently re-checking the cited commit hashes and file:line references against the actual repository — before submission. \n Proof of concept \n Versions tested \n Built from source at current HEAD 9ae75235a (2026-10-02). Source also read statically at the 3.29.0 tag (first release claiming the fix) and confirmed byte-identical for the relevant logic. \n Reproduction \n Built libfreerdp3.so from the real, unmodified FreeRDP source (one 10-line pass-through wrapper added only to export the otherwise-internal-linkage tls_verify_certificate() symbol for direct testing — no logic changed). Loaded real X.509 certificates via FreeRDP's own freerdp_certificate_new_from_file() / x509_utils_from_pem() , verified chain trust via FreeRDP's own freerdp_certificate_verify() (real OpenSSL X509_verify_cert() against a locally-trusted test CA installed via the standard X509_LOOKUP_hash_dir layout FreeRDP itself uses), then called the real tls_verify_certificate() with settings matching the original advisory's own stated repro config ( AutoAcceptCertificate=FALSE , AutoDenyCertificate=TRUE , AuthenticationLevel=2 ). \n Case 2 (CN-fallback). Leaf cert: CN=victim.test , SAN= DNS:attacker.test (non-matching), signed by the trusted test CA. Target hostname: victim.test . \n \n Result: certificate_status=1 (chain trusted), tls_verify_certificate() → verification_status=1 — silent accept, no deny-path log, despite the only SAN entry not matching. \n \n Case 3 (IP-literal). Leaf cert: CN=ignored-cn.test , SAN= DNS:127.0.0.1 (a plain DNS-type SAN string, not a real iPAddress SAN). Target: 127.0.0.1 . \n \n Result: certificate_status=1 , verification_status=1 — silent accept. \n \n Supplementary. A cert with a genuine IP:127.0.0.1 iPAddress SAN entry (no DNS SAN) returned x509_utils_get_dns_names() → count=0 — confirming at runtime, not just by reading the switch statement, that FreeRDP's extraction code cannot see true iPAddress SAN entries at all. \n Controls (same mechanism, flip only the value the suspected logic consumes): Case 2 control — identical SAN structure ( DNS:attacker.test , non-matching), but CN=something-else.test (also non-matching) → verification_status=-1 , real deny-path log [tls_verify_certificate]: No certificate stored, automatically denying. fired. Case 3 control — SAN=DNS:10.10.10.10 instead of the target IP → verification_status=-1 . Both controls confirm the positive results are caused specifically by the CN/SAN fallback logic, not by chain-trust or some unrelated acceptance path. \n Stack / environment reach \n Verification level: called FreeRDP's real, unmodified tls_verify_certificate() directly with certificates loaded and chain-verified through FreeRDP's own loader and OpenSSL verification code — the same objects the function receives after a genuine client TLS handshake (confirmed by reading the real call site at tls.c:1093 ). A full network handshake through an actual TCP proxy / xfreerdp CLI invocation was not performed, since the vulnerable logic operates purely on an already-parsed certificate object and a hostname string, independent of the transport layer. \n Verification level \n Reproduced by calling FreeRDP's real verification function directly on real, OpenSSL-chain-verified X.509 certificates, with isolating control groups for both cases (not merely observed in source, and not a full end-to-end network handshake). \n Impact \n A man-in-the-middle able to present a certificate that chains to a CA the FreeRDP client already trusts can silently bypass FreeRDP's server-identity verification for RDP connections, enabling full confidentiality and integrity compromise of the session (screen content, clipboard, keystrokes, and the ability to inject input) with no client-side prompt or warning — AutoDenyCertificate=TRUE is never consulted because hostname_match is wrongly TRUE. This reproduces, unchanged, the exact two of three cases the original advisory ( GHSA-43hh-p3vw-hfx3 ) already documented and whose metadata states were fixed in 3.29.0. Precondition: the attacker must hold a certificate trusted via the target's existing trust-chain configuration (misissuance, a compromised/rogue CA, or a private-CA environment) — this is not a no-precondition bypass of a validly-issued, correctly-scoped certificate. Happy to follow whatever coordinated-disclosure timeline works for the team, and to verify against a candidate patch. \n \n \n\n \n\n \n \n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n \n \n Severity \n \n \n High\n \n\n \n \n \n 7.4 \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n CVSS overall score\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n This score calculates overall vulnerability severity from 0 to 10 and is based on the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS). \n \n \n \n \n / 10 \n \n \n CVSS v3 base metrics \n\n \n Attack vector\n Network \n \n Attack complexity\n High \n \n Privileges required\n None \n \n User interaction\n None \n \n Scope\n Unchanged \n \n Confidentiality\n High \n \n Integrity\n High \n \n Availability\n None \n \n \n \n Learn more about base metrics \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n CVSS v3 base metrics\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Attack vector: \n More severe the more the remote (logically and physically) an attacker can be in order to exploit the vulnerability. \n \n \n Attack complexity: \n More severe for the least complex attacks. \n \n \n Privileges required: \n More severe if no privileges are required. \n \n \n User interaction: \n More severe when no user interaction is required. \n \n \n Scope: \n More severe when a scope change occurs, e.g. one vulnerable component impacts resources in components beyond its security scope. \n \n \n Confidentiality: \n More severe when loss of data confidentiality is highest, measuring the level of data access available to an unauthorized user. \n \n \n Integrity: \n More severe when loss of data integrity is the highest, measuring the consequence of data modification possible by an unauthorized user. \n \n \n Availability: \n More severe when the loss of impacted component availability is highest. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:N \n\n \n\n \n \n CVE ID \n \n No known CVE\n \n \n\n \n Weaknesses \n \n \n Weakness \n CWE-295 \n \n \n \n Improper Certificate Validation \n The product does not validate, or incorrectly validates, a certificate.\n Learn more on MITRE. \n\n \n \n\n \n\n \n\n\n\n \n\n \n \n\n\n \n\n \n\n \n \n\n \n Footer \n\n \n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n &copy; 2026 GitHub, Inc.\n \n \n\n \n Footer navigation \n\n \n\n\n \n Terms \n \n\n \n Privacy \n \n\n\n \n Security \n \n\n \n Status \n \n\n \n Community \n \n\n \n Docs \n \n\n \n Contact \n \n\n \n \n \n \n Manage cookies\n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n Do not share my personal information\n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n You can’t perform that action at this time.\n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n\n\n\n \n \n \n \n \n"
