inal PoS alarm. reply gambiting 15 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n My mum has a Nissan X-Trail, it does the same thing. reply PaulHoule 15 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n There's a certain sense in which competence in some domains repels competence in other domains. If Apple made a car the rear fender would come off just as you opened the drive side door. If I had to hazard a guess as to the mechanism as it would turn up in an automotive company it is that automotive engineers have the SAE to set professional standards and that software \"engineers\" have nothing [1]... And if an organized group has a fight with a disorganized group the result is predictable. [1] the ACM is a professional associate for computer science professors. reply HeWhoLurksLate 13 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > There's a certain sense in which competence in some domains repels competence in other domains. If Apple made a car the rear fender would come off just as you opened the drive side door. This feels like a logical fallacy but I'm having a terrible time disproving it at anything near large company scale reply rightnutwingjob 11 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n It’s the Homoeostasis of Barely Tolerable Design. reply flanz_10 15 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n > I don't understand how Subaru stays in the business. Just how. It's a 4 digit time. Just broadcast it on CAN. Why not. they sell SKUs with no nav and no remote display for the nav. and they still need a clock. reply 306bobby 11 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I don't see how that changes the argument? Still broadcast the time to the ether reply olyjohn 6 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n But then the base model with only one clock would need a clock that reads the can bus, and something putting the time on the CAN bus. Instead they just put a cheap ass standalone clock in the base model. And this clock trickles up to the models with more features. reply numpad0 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n IIRC at least one of the two other displays optionally take turn-by-turn remote guidances from the nav. So it can easily have a time data type added to that data, and they didn't. Though, all I researched for was for an outdated model of a car, and it seems one of three desynched clocks appear to be not be present anymore, and the desynced clock count must have gone down by one, unless they compensated for the loss by adding one. reply loeg 18 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n And their engines have struggled with gasket issues and oil consumption for a long time. People here love the AWD, though (my state enshrines AWD as a special class in law, artificially driving some demand). reply clickety_clack 18 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n That makes sense. AWD handling is so much better in winter conditions that I feel it has to make it less likely to crash. reply hansvm 12 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n This is a pretty common misconception (it's worse with 4WD, since at least with AWD you sometimes have torque vectoring and other features which do improve your ability to stay on the road while accelerating), and it gets a lot of people hurt. In most reasonable scenarios (not doing anything dumb like accelerating through the curve), you're more likely to fly off a winter curve with AWD than FWD. The big advantage is when accelerating. It's a nice quality of life feature. I typically switch to AWD when I move to winter climates. It's not as safe though, _especially_ if you have little enough training that you're relying on intuition to make that conclusion that AWD must be safer. reply normie3000 10 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > not doing anything dumb like accelerating through the curve Is this dumb in winter, or dumb generally? I was taught to do this. reply hansvm 9 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Sorry, it's hard to capture all the nuance a conversation like this needs without writing so much that the length keeps people from capturing the important parts. If you want to maximize speed through a curve plus straightaway, you should pick a path through that curve minimizing curvature at the end so that you are able to accelerate out the end of the curve. As a reasonably good approximation for normal driving, your tires have the same traction regardless of the direction of the load being placed on them. That gives you a budget of net force you're able to apply. If you're near the sideways force limit going around a curve and add acceleration (or braking -- always do your braking before you enter the turn, or gradually reduce it as the curvature increases), the wheels with those extra forces will break traction. The solution is to apply that acceleration at a point of milder curvature. Taking that idea back down to safe driving speeds, the same idea exists when considering crossing a margin of error rather than the ultimate slip threshold. All else equal, accelerating as you come out of a curve, if you've chosen your path through the curve correctly (really important to get that right; there's a curve back near my hometown which has taken out half a dozen people over the years because its curvature increases at the end and they haven't planned for it), will minimize your time through the curve and the following straightaway at your chosen safety threshold. However, all of that is predicated on minimizing time assuming perfect knowledge and perfect driving. The acceleration itself is actively harmful from a safety perspective. In FWD, the wheels losing traction are the ones connected to your steering wheel. That's not ideal. In RWD you're prone to oversteer and spinning out of control. In AWD you can get either or both of those failure modes. Moreover, navigating typical curves, you're so far from slippage that the technique of accelerating at the end just means you'll go from the speed limit to something greater. For winter curves, the margin of traction uncertainty is so great that you can't gain much from accelerating out the end anyway (and have a pretty bad risk profile -- accelerate on a straightaway and slip a bit and with a bit of training you're basically fine, slip off a curve and you have a really bad rest of your short life potentially). Safely accelerating in a curve is a learned skill though, and most people don't care that much about driving. Unless it looks like they're interested in more depth, my advice is always to brake before the curve and accelerate afterward. It's safe, fast enough, and easy to remember. reply justsomehnguy 10 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n A powered rear axle tries to overcome the front. This why you can do 'drifting' on a RWD. And when you are in the curve there are different forces and road resistance on your front and rear axles (your outer side is loaded and inner is not) so if you do accelerate on a 4WD/AWD you are asking your car to spin out. reply olyjohn 6 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Meh. FWD solves this. AWD is good for getting going when you're stopped, but other than that it doesn't help much. reply red369 6 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I think this is true, but there are still reasons to like RWD: - Smaller turning circle because the front wheels can turn further without complicated engineering. - More traction when accelerating on a wet surface, due to weight transfer backwards. (1) - Usually better weight distribution due to engine layout. There are other downsides in addition to what you mentioned: - Any scenario where you will definitely lose traction, then rear-wheel drive becomes harder to control. There was an old Top Gear episode with a steep grassy hill, and drivers of the RWD cars could not get up it. (2) - The transmission tunnel required for RWD - Reduced fuel economy due to having to turn the power 90 degrees (assuming the engine is mounted in the front). (3) 1) This one may sound like unnecessary acceleration, and it probably often is, but there are situations where it can arise, especially if you prefer older cars. There is one particular hill I used to encounter frequently, where you are trying to turn onto a main road, so you want to accelerate fairly briskly to avoid waiting for a huge gap in traffic. On economy tires, in the wet, you can easily reach the limits of FWD. I would see many cars time a gap, and then have to rapidly decide to give up on it when their acceleration was much slower than they expected due to wheel spin. I think there may have been bus lane paint on the road too, but I'm not certain. Obviously AWD is even better than RWD for traction, but at that point, that is usually only when requiring rapid acceleration. 2) I suppose with perfect/computer assisted driving, RWD would out-perform FWD. 3) I once searched, and had a very hard time quantifying this, but it is almost certainly a few percent, and perhaps more. There are also weight differences involved because of the long drive-shaft, but I think the effect is larger than that. It is very hard to find an apples-to-apples comparison. reply hbs18 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n RWD cars also feel much nicer to steer, even when just driving around town. The fact that the steering axle doesn't receive engine torque makes working the steering wheel while moving feel smoother and less jerky. reply 0xbadcafebee 12 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n AWD provides more traction while accelerating, which makes it safer, which is why people use it in winter... Saying it's less safe is ridiculous. Now if you happen to be really good at driving, it's even safer because you can recover from a slide faster, again because of the increased traction during acceleration. Once your tires have traction again the additional traction moves you out of the slide faster. The only problem here is people going too fast which can happen with any car. reply hansvm 8 hours ago | root | parent | next [