one cycle per second. It'd take about 2.5 days to complete a one-hour change via this mechanism. reply skipkey 19 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n I don’t know about that, but I was told by someone who worked at a power generation plant in the 1970s that they had a clock in the plant, that they would compare to the time broadcast on the radio frequency that carried the official time, and any time it diverged they would slightly adjust the frequency of the alternating current until it matched. reply yardie 18 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I was a kid but I do know the clocks in my elementary, middle, and high school were accurate. Near end of day, we would impatiently stare at the clock waiting for the release bell. Being a public school, the clocks were bolted securely to the wall. Being little shits, students would probably steal them if they could, LOL. So how did they keep 100s of clocks on time? I asked and was told by the school maintenance that it was done through power. reply ButlerianJihad 3 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Master_clock reply jandrese 17 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n I remember in my elementary school the clocks were all synchronized to a system somewhere, presumably in the head office. The day after going back to school after a DST change the clocks would all be off by an hour and sometime in that morning they would all suddenly freeze, then fast forward or rewind an hour and then restart. In retrospect it is amusing that whomever was in charge of updating the clocks didn't do it when they first got in but waited until the school day had started. reply EvanAnderson 20 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n It's crazy they didn't have a centralized clock system in a facility like that. What a thing to scrimp on. reply jandrese 17 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n What are the odds they did have a centralized system for setting the clocks, but n