ernational shipping, parts are cheaper from there than locally. reply FireBeyond 14 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n > My coworker drives a much newer (2026) SUV from VW... Apparently the button became like 4 screens in the touch-screen tablet the car comes with, just to force people to stop hitting the physical button, and you can't just disable it permanently. > Well.. unless you jailbreak the software Maybe this is locked on VW, but given that Audi and Porsche share a lot of the same software, methodology, etc., I feel \"decently confident\" that this can be disabled via an OBD scanner (obdEleven, for one, was built around the VAG brands). I was able to disable it in my fiancée's entry trim 2017 A4 in about 20 seconds, and no manual \"long codes\". It's not an option needed on my RS 5, but I've done a few other things via the tool, and the dealership didn't have an issue with it - when I brought my car in for some other issue (water ingress into a sensor/light), all they said was \"Are you okay with the tech resetting any changes you've made if they need to for diagnostics?\" (they didn't), and \"Do you accept that there will be a diagnostic fee if the tech doing a reset of changes resolves your problem?\" reply ithiru 9 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I also hate when my android based dash can't stay when I mute, every time I start it will unmute and with the same volume but FM, thought of doing similar to this.. right time. reply encom 19 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I wish someone would jailbreak the Nissan Leaf (MY2018) head unit. Every time I turn on the car, it shows a EULA asking if Nissan can please collect every detail of my car at all times and sell it to whoever asks. Every. Time. Even if I beat my head with hammers to think like a lawyer, I can't imagine how it would be necessary to ask constantly. Doesn't matter if I accept or decline, it still asks again. reply properbrew 17 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n I hate to laugh but the thought of you constantly rejecting the EULA for weeks on end, to finally give in one day, press accept and come to terms with it, only to get in the next time and have it pop up. I can only imagine the amount of expletives that were said at that exact moment. God, I hate modern cars. reply encom 7 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n >God, I hate modern cars. Me too, but it's more a love/hate relationship, because aside from this issue, and a few other annoyances, I love this car to bits. I've been driving for over 25 years, and this car is my favourite of all of them, including some newer and fancier electrics. I think it's the throttle response. Nissan just nailed it. reply unstatusthequo 12 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I bought a Porsche GT3 coded to Canada. Meaning km/h in speedometer in instrument cluster, etc. Dealers had no clue now to recode. Porsche NA also not helpful. Russian car forums for the win. Recoded myself in a few hours for only the cost of a usb to OBD-II adapter. reply fefaOd 19 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n The French auto industry... has always been \"Special\" reply neuroelectron 14 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n My 2008 Honda Civic has a old-fashioned set-it style clock, but from the factory, it's always ran about a minute a week fast. Which of a course is an excruciating kind of boiling the frog. Also, it now sometimes thinks my headlights are on and dims the display for a few seconds randomly which I guess isn't too bad after 15+ years. It doesn't seem to have any other problems except for worn out suspension and leaking exhaust. reply GrumpySciGuy 15 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Love it! reply tuberreact 18 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n an SD card requirement is crazy reply very_good_man 18 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Nice! Reminds me of my jailbreaking my car's computer to write a string to permanent memory https://www.josephecombs.com/2026/09/08/coding-an-audi-batte... reply fHr 19 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Shitboxing ftw reply fHr 19 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Meanwhile me with shitbox reply lifestyleguru 19 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n 8 inches or more of dashboard real estate to display \"maps not found\", \"don't get distracted\" warning, terms and conditions, and blinking \"12:00\". Having had rented a fair share of subcompact and compact cars in half dozen of countries, the car navigation was updated and working exactly only once. reply ForHackernews 20 hours ago | prev [–] \n > The system had DRM checks which rejected SD cards without the correct CID (a unique ID burned into the card) and files with signatures based on that specific CID, so I couldn't even copy the files to a blank SD card. FWIW, there are grey market sites that will sell you an SD burned with a custom CID so you can clone an SD card with this kind of copy protection. It sounds like in this case maybe he could've gotten away with an empty maps card + menu definitions file. reply hackernudes 12 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n I bet you can make a microcontroller emulate an SD card like that too. reply uyjulian 19 hours ago | parent | prev [–] \n There is code execution exploit in Samsung SD/eMMC firmware which, among other things, will allow changing the CID reply \n \n Guidelines | FAQ | Lists | API | Security | Legal | Apply to YC | Contact \n Search: "
