 "page:https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50360164": {
  "v": 4,
  "fetched_at": "2026-11-04T12:48:35.397Z",
  "bytes": 92124,
  "text": " C++26 Disapproved | Hacker News Hacker News new | past | comments | ask | show | jobs | submit login C++26 Disapproved ( andreasfertig.com ) 337 points by thatxliner 12 hours ago | hide | past | favorite | 230 comments help \n \n lewdwig 0 minutes ago | next [–] \n Maybe C++ overthought the generality of the feature and under-delivered on the MVP (a common committee failure mode). Two keywords such as \"always-enforce\" and \"build-configurable\" for assertions would have covered the vast majority of what people wanted from contracts. reply jandrewrogers 8 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n As someone who writes high-reliability C++ but is completely outside the standards process, I see the issues with C++ Contracts. It isn’t clear that it solves a problem that anyone has in real codebases, especially after C++20 concepts. If you were already writing high-reliability C++ then the benefits are difficult to quantify. If you weren’t writing high-reliability C++ then it saves you almost nothing over doing the standard idioms of high-reliability C++ that were already available to you at very marginally more cost. It does standardize expression of it in narrow cases but the payoff seems minimal. Most of what C++ Contracts delivers is a mashup of what you can already do with constexpr and C++20 Concepts. I like reliability features but this feature, as delivered, looks fairly useless if you already use reliability features. A compelling standard would need to make much deeper guarantees out of the box. And the compiler can already see through most compositions of constexpr and C++20 concepts, which is already very expressive. C++ Contracts looks a bit like a feature that was designed long before current standard features existed. reply throwaway2037 5 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n > high-reliability\n \nI never heard this term used with C++ before. Can you explain more? Ideally: Can you give an example snip of code that demonstrates it clearly? reply zaphirplane 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Top 2 Google search result are ..: this post and this article reply Rexxar 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Google give enormous priority to new content, especially on very well referenced and active sites like HN. So it probably doesn't mean as much as you think. reply arein3 4 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Its a set of rules, used by defense software, they are available on the internet reply weitendorf 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I think you're thinking of https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/High_Integrity_C%2B%2B I wrote a comment explaining what in my mind distinguishes \"high reliability\" (defensive programming and making invariants/error cases/intended use explicit) C++ from regular C++. IMO it basically boils down to contemporary \"Google/fintech style C++\", which is a different development culture and ecosystem from defense/automotive reply fpoling 28 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n The biggest split is not using exceptions with aborts on memory overflow. It alters the programming style to such extent that it feels like a different language. reply NSUserDefaults 3 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n For example the failed F-35 project used it. Seems pretentious and risky compared to using a language that is safe by default. reply Cthulhu_ 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n What language do you propose? (I'll just pre-empt that if you say \"Rust\", Rust wasn't around until recently and the F-35 project started in '93. These things span decades, while only a few programming languages do) reply LiamPowell 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n The incredibly obvious answer is Ada, given the DoD commissioned it and the fact that it was used for the F-22. reply fpoling 31 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I wonder if DoD desire to tap into a bigger pool of C++ programmers also brought a lot of people who was not trained to write a high-reliability code. reply tialaramex 2 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n C++ is a pre-standard \"Eh, just do whatever\" language in 1993. Bjarne's 2005 JSF rules have the same effect as an \"Employees must wash hands\" sign. It's not the billion dollar fast food chain's fault that a minimum wage employee didn't obey the sign and now you've got food-borne illness. They put a sign up! reply amelius 3 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n > compared to a language that is safe by default. This also sounds pretentious ... reply NSUserDefaults 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Because you think there is no such language, or even one that gets closer than C++? Look I still kind of like C++… for games. I would never put it in a fighter jet for gods sake. reply fps-hero 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I don’t want to be inflammatory, so let’s stick to facts. The F35 is the manifestation of a test aircraft designed in the 90s. Of that era, software was predominantly single threaded, hardware definitely so. They also weren’t using the latest and greatest of hardware at the time, they opted for proven flight platforms with hardware dating back well back into the decade. So, very lacklustre performance, tight memory constraints. The only other legitimate choice for writing software at the time would have been ADA, which for some reason never took off despite being purpose build for the application. Of that time, C++ was the new hotness, it was popular, it was solving architectural problems, and it was performant, unlike say Java. It’s fairly naive to say “I’d never use C++”. The C++ programmers of the time would have looked at Fortran or COBOL with the same level of disdain. We are lucky to live in a time of better options, and the impending post code future. reply NSUserDefaults 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n “Let’s use the new hotness for this new fighter plane” maybe puts things into perspective for me. In today's terms, that's like vibecoding a Mars mission vehicle just because it's \"cool\". reply tialaramex 2 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n > The only other legitimate choice for writing software at the time would have been ADA Ada makes a lot more sense for this work than C++ did twenty years ago. reply drysine 1 hour ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n >it [C++] was solving architectural problems What do you mean? reply weitendorf 3 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Assuming the original commenter is referring to essentially best-practices, defensive C++ like you see at Google and projects like Envoy proxy, and the other reply is prob mixxed up and thinking of high integrity C++ [0], Here's an example that uses compile time type assertions (static_assert, C++ can already do this), runtime errors (CHECK, standard Google/abseil practice for explicitly handling invalid states and invariants), test/dev checks (defensive programming, basically an inline test), and wrapper/error handling types to do what you might want contracts to do: template <typename T>\n absl::StatusOr<double> Percentile(std::span<const T> sorted_values, double p) {\n static_assert(std::is_arithmetic_v<T>, \"T must be numeric\");\n CHECK(p >= 0.0 && p <= 100.0) << \"p out of range: \" << p;\n DCHECK(std::ranges::is_sorted(sorted_values)) << \"input not sorted\";\n\n if (sorted_values.empty()) {\n return absl::InvalidArgumentError(\"empty input\");\n }\n\n const std::size_t last = sorted_values.size() - 1;\n const double rank = p / 100.0 * last;\n const auto lo = static_cast<std::size_t>(rank);\n const std::size_t hi = std::min(lo + 1, last);\n const double result = std::lerp<double>(sorted_values[lo], sorted_values[hi],\n rank - lo);\n\n DCHECK(result >= sorted_values.front() && result <= sorted_values.back());\n return result;\n }\n \n(function impl is AI, only need it as scaffolding to demonstrate the contract-adjacent, non-implementation code) Before I was exposed to this style of C++ and learned to write it, C++ basically felt like a different language. People say that the C++ standards committee and language designers have different priorities and developer culture vs most big tech companies/finance and I think it's because even if they're aware of the features and tools that come out of Google (et al) [1], they don't use them in their current day to day jobs and want that functionality in the core C++ toolchain. IMO, a lot of what they want to add is already \"solved\" by third party tooling, but because for whatever reason they don't use those as part of their everyday work, they sometimes come up with language features that overcomplicate or solve the wrong thing, in a way that you would intuitively understand if you were already regularly using the 3P tools that solve them. I could also be the one misinterpreting things here so apologies if this is obvious or not what the original commenter meant by \"high reliability\". [0] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/High_Integrity_C%2B%2B [1] https://abseil.io/about/ one of many third party ways to make C++ less insane reply pedro_caetano 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n If you squint hard enough your example does look a lot like Ada pre and post condition contracts (perhaps a bit less ergonomic, at least for someone who hasn't written C++ in a while). At this point the rust contracts crate with requires/ensures looks like a more straightforward approximation of Ada's Contracts and assertion policy. But still as far as I am aware no one has implemented something that mimics the static proof functionality in Ada's SPARK subset (which arguably not all applications or problem domains actually need or can justify the extra effort/cost). reply drivebyhooting 8 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Your comment just made me realize contracts is different from concepts (which are basically bounded genetics, right…?) reply jandrewrogers 8 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n C++ concepts evaluate the properties of types at compile-time. You can inject arbitrary constraints on type properties anywhere in the code including conditional codegen. It is a way of asking if a type “can express this complex set of abstract things or has this enumerated set of properties” with almost arbitrary detail. They are extremely powerful in terms of expressiveness and specialization of code. Insanely good for performance and reliability engineering. One of the best features to be added to C++ in many years. C++ contracts are an attempt to bring C asserts into the C++ age. The weakness of C++ contracts is that they are mostly syntactic sugar over C asserts. If you really care about this type of thing, you probably already have a custom C++ assertion framework that is at least as capable if not more. The promise of C++ contracts is that they can aid formal verification, which would be great. As delivered, it can’t do much more than was already possible, which will inhibit adoption. reply amluto 7 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I maintain a codebase that I’ve migrated from C++98 to C++11 and all the way to C++23 and even a bit of draft 26 (the latter for some minor library improvements). Few things made me quite as happy as moving from using SFINAE to using requires clauses and concepts. (IMO the single best improvements over the years have been atomics and maybe variadic templates.) reply jandrewrogers 7 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I think you are right to position atomics and variadic templates as among the most consequential features of C++ in the last couple decades. It made an enormous number of things possible. One thing I give the C++ feature gods credit for is recognizing material deficiencies in systems languages and unapologetically trying to fill those gaps starting with C++11. The addition of atomics, strict variadics, constexpr, lambdas, concepts, lifetime annotations, etc has been transformative for C++. reply throwaway2037 4 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n > the single best improvements over the years have been atomics...\n \nOf course, libatomic and friends have existed for a long time. Real question: Do you feel that the standardisation of atomics within the C++ base library is a material advantage over using libatomic? Also, maybe there are atomic APIs in the C++ base library that are better than libatomic. If so, please kindly share an example or two. I would like to learn more. EDIT I forgot to give a hat tip to your big upgrade from C++98 to C++23. In some projects, that can require an enormous amount of effort! Can you share a \"stories from the trenches\" that were difficult in that upgrade? reply pocksuppet 4 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n libatomic is a GCC implementation detail that implements atomic math operations that are not available on the target platform by using non-atomic math and compare-exchange. Are you sure that's the one you mean? reply Shorel 4 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n I want to read your blog describing these experiences. reply AlotOfReading 8 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n The weakness of C++ contracts is that they are mostly syntactic sugar over C asserts. \n \nThe sugar is actually one of my issues with them. The evaluation semantics are much more complicated / potentially slower than C asserts because of the sugar, and why you would have to pay those costs isn't obvious at first glance. Lisa Lippincott's excellent talk on the design of contracts [0] is what switched me from a sort of neutral support to disliking it. I think it's a stupid place for the national bodies to draw the line though. std::regex is acceptable, but a nothingburger like contracts tanks the vote? [0] https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yhhSW-FSWkE reply jandrewrogers 8 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n To be clear, I have a lot of issues with the contracts proposal. I probably wouldn’t use despite living out on the standards frontier. I don’t even use C asserts already because they are not expressive enough in important ways. This proposal kind of reifies the inadequacies. I think the risk is that C++ contracts become an evolutionary dead-end that hang around forever in code bases because they were standardized. It wouldn’t be the first time this has happened. reply randomNumber7 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n What do you do instead of C asserts? Throwing exceptions? reply stingraycharles 8 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Concepts are evaluated compile time and make a lot of TMP more elegant. Contracts are runtime check, akin to assertions. Phrased differently, contracts are to concepts what assert is to static_assert. reply rerdavies 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I think the wumpus that's being chased is that contracts COULD evolve into a compile time check. I'd be much happier if they actually caught the wumpus before they start polluting the language with yet another baroquely complicated feature that gets abandoned before it actually becomes useful (e.g. coroutines, and modules). Or just keep it simple and give us a C++-sensible replacement for assert(), and avoid the rest of it. i.e. contract_assert without the word \"contract\" in it. That would be a nice feature. reply MFHava 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Funny how often I read online that Coroutines are abandoned/useless, yet we ship products based on them and their application in our codebases grows continuously... reply mikepurvis 6 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Too bad it couldn't have been contract and static_contract. reply YuechenLi 7 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n A concept is a compile time requirement that bounds templates which are the generics in C++, so you are close. reply rramadass 8 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Concepts are for Types at compile time while Contracts are for Values at runtime. reply pjmlp 5 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n The problem is that the current design doesn't take into account binary libraries, or what happens when conflicting contract modes are linked together, as contracts aren't reflected on ABI. Additionally they offer very little mechanisms versus Eiffel's original design, Ada/SPARK or Frama-C. A basic MVP, expected to improve on subsequent C++ version, assuming that the proposal authors stick around for those evolution proposals. reply quotemstr 8 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Huh? Concepts are types of types. Contracts are fancier, structured runtime assertions. What does one have to do with the other? They're nothing alike. reply taybin 8 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n They both start with the letter C, have two syllables, and have/had a hard time getting into the standard. If you’re not paying close attention, it’s easy to get them confused. reply moomin 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I’ll admit I only learned they were different today. I’d like to think that if I were a regular C++ user I’d be on top of this, though. reply jandrewrogers 8 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n That’s exactly my point. Contracts notionally offer the ability to lift some runtime assertions to compile-time. That is how it was sold. Except they don’t in practice. Meanwhile, concepts + constexpr if used artfully deliver that in practice, including for many cases that contracts were supposed to solve. I push all verification to compile-time to the extent possible. reply quotemstr 6 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I don't buy the idea that in \"high-reliability C++\" every condition worth checking can be checked statically, not unless you define \"high-reliability\" vacuously as that set of programs for which the claim is true. Real-world programs have legitimate reasons to verify invariants dynamically, and C++26 contracts provides an improved way to do so. While the C++26 variant of contracts doesn't provide the benefits of its static-analysis-centric ancestor from 2014, and therefore isn't as consequential a feature, it still provides value to some and is absolutely not important enough to be worth killing a standard. Concepts do not provide this value. In any case, it's simply false that C++26 contracts are unnecessary because we have concepts, yet this claim is the thrust of your initial comment. reply jandrewrogers 6 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n The issue is that contracts are less flexible than the existing capabilities in practice, it is limited syntactic sugar. Contracts were sold with the idea of aiding compile-time verification, a noble purpose. The combination of concepts + constexpr allows you to do deeper compile-time verification today by other means than contracts can check. It isn’t obvious that this will ever not be the case. This means contracts is relegated to being another flavor of C asserts. C++26 contracts are arguably unnecessary because we can achieve the same results other ways, sometimes with stronger properties. I’m a proponent of features that clean up the syntax but C++ contracts look like a nod to people that still use legacy C asserts. That’s fine but it also perpetuates an inadequate model of what assertions should be capable of expressing. People figured out how to do with C++20 better than what contracts promised. reply quotemstr 6 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n There's nothing \"legacy\" about checking invariants at runtime, and you can't simultaneously dismiss the concerns of people doing so on the grounds that they're \"still us[ing] legacy C asserts\" when you deny them an alternative to \"legacy C asserts\". That's question-begging. > People figured out how to do with C++20 better than what contracts promised. What contracts used to promise is irrelevant. The contracts on the table modernized runtime assertions and are irrelevant to concepts, constexpr, and related facilities. Is the contracts feature more useful than C's assert()? If you want to argue that we're better off with the old macro, do so directly. Concepts, in this context, amount to irrelevant distraction. reply jandrewrogers 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I have no issues with people check invariants at runtime. It is unavoidable. C++ contracts is a mediocre way of doing it. Many C++ code bases don’t use any macros at all for assertions and haven’t for a long time. Their models are much more nuanced than C++ contracts allows. Why would anyone that already ditched C asserts use C++ contracts when it can’t express what the alternatives already can? This isn’t a new problem. People were creating more expressive assertion replacements than contracts offers a decade ago. C++ contracts are nerfed. It is less expressive and capable than what anyone that cares about these things already uses. So who is expected to use it? reply rramadass 26 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > Why would anyone that already ditched C asserts use C++ contracts when it can’t express what the alternatives already can? You have not understood the C++26 contracts proposal. It introduces three new syntax viz. \"Pre\" and \"Post\" in function signature declaration and a separate \"contract_assert\" which you can use anywhere in the code path. It is the \"Pre\"/\"Post\" that is useful here. Since it is part of function signature declaration, a static analyzer can easily make sure that all function calls satisfy the contract else you get warning/error. The compiler on its part can inject appropriate code at the function boundary points to do runtime checking etc. You cannot do this with assert macros. If you wanted something like this earlier, you would have had to change the compiler to emit modified function prologue/epilogue and build your own contract framework which few people had the capability to do. You can now see the immediate benefits particularly for standard library (and other) code hardening needs. Many UB circumstances can be easily flagged at compile time without too much complexity. Finally, note that if you use compile-time switch \"ignore\" to turn-off everything, only the runtime checks are elided (so you do not lose any performance) but the static analyzer can still catch all possible inconsistencies at compile-time thus giving you the best of both worlds. reply rramadass 6 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n > While the C++26 variant of contracts doesn't provide the benefits of its static-analysis-centric ancestor from 2014 Do you have any information as to why they shifted the focus from static analysis to runtime checks? I only remember that it was based on some Microsoft annotation language or something. reply rramadass 8 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Absolutely Right! - https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50361435 reply jibalt 7 hours ago | parent | prev | next [2 more] \n [flagged] locknitpicker 7 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > The problem is that a bunch of people who didn't care for them did something very extreme by voting against approving C++26. The only extreme and tone deaf thing I see in this story is how contracts proponents were faced with multiple papers demonstrating quite clearly and extensively how they were broken in specification and in form, but still tried to ignore all the criticism and force them through a consensus process in spite of being clearly opposed. How does this make any sense? What was the plan here? To blackmail the C++ community to accept a broken feature under threat of the proposal not passing? Make it make sense? What I want to see is the people who tried to force contacts upon the process in spite of all opposition to come forth and explain their rationale. It was highly absurd and even unthinkable to force a broken feature down C++'s throats with bureaucratic chicanery, and then complain that the same bureaucracy has mechanisms to stop it's abuse. I for one would like to hear an explanation. reply nanolith 7 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n While I can understand that C++ contracts were met with controversy, contract annotations scoped at analysis time that can be verified by external tools, such as static analyzers, model checkers, or automatic theorem provers, are incredibly useful. I put together an alternative function contract and invariant mechanism in C using CBMC and custom macros that effectively allows me to perform formal methods in C using compositional verification. The reason why I chose C is because of its relative simplicity and relative ease of building tooling around it. C++ contracts provide some feature parity, and would let me take advantage of some of the features in C++ relative to C. I hope that the contract proposal is workshopped and simplified so it can be proposed again for a future version. Its current form is unsuitable, but it deserves a second chance with a simpler implementation. Contracts would narrow the safety gap between C++ and languages such as Ada / SPARK. reply uecker 5 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n C and formal verification is an awesome combination. I tried cmbc on some toy programs, it it fully automatically proved memory safety and all assertions. But for larger programs one has to split up with a contract-like facility. Can you tell us more how you use cbmc with macros? I am also exploring this. I also wonder what feature from C++ you miss in C? reply Surac 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Well i you ask me i miss namespaces and functions i could put into struct and enum types reply locknitpicker 1 hour ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n > contract annotations scoped at analysis time that can be verified by external tools, such as static analyzers, model checkers, or automatic theorem provers, are incredibly useful. Yes they would be. Except that's not the contracts that they tried to force down C++'s throats. The current contracts proposals features things like throwing exceptions in contract predicates, leaves out basic features such as function pointers, and not to mention that the contract specification alone covers around 5-10% of C++ specification and still glances over most details as being implementation defined. As everyone is attacking Stroustrup as this master mind behind this opposition, perhaps people should devote some time to read one of the many papers pointing out the many flaws in the proposal. Stroustrup himself features as the coauthor of a few of them. Why is this not a technical discussion but a witch hunt? reply twhitmore 6 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Contracts are useful, but don't guaranteed anything if type safety & memory safety can be bypassed. I think the ISO panel made the right call. The real world consider memory safety & execution integrity to already be critical issues (and C++ no longer to be viable), and they are correct. The WG has its priorities wrong. reply uecker 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Memory safety can also be bypassed in Rust. If you want something where memory safety is guaranteed, use Fil-C but this comes at a performance cost. Or use formal verification, where then contracts can help (but C++'s complexity doesn't). reply LugosFergus 9 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I get why some folks would like to see contracts in the language, but C++ is also buckling due to the shear complexity that's getting tacked on to it. I'd rather see more proposals such as C++26's \"erroneous behavior\" that attempts to reign in the language's shortcomings. reply airstrafer 8 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Agree. There's an argument that Contracts reduces the complexity of using the language because it offers a more readable alternative to template metaprogramming (also, it has better error messages). But the argument really falls apart when you review WG21's track record for other features. Like for example, Coroutines were billed as an easier way to write concurrent code, but personally I think it completely failed and ended up being hopelessly complex and confusing. Modules are the same - superficially they make things simpler, but when people actually go to adopt it, the details are so complex that nobody bothers. reply pjmlp 4 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Some do bother, e.g. Microsoft Office uses modules, there is a talk on it. reply kllrnohj 7 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n I think you're confusing it with Concepts. Contracts are basically just asserts, nothing to do with templates. reply locknitpicker 6 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n > There's an argument that Contracts reduces the complexity of using the language because it offers a more readable alternative to template metaprogramming Like most people in this thread, you should really read up on contracts before commenting. The contracts proposal is for runtime assertions, and their problem is that they are poorly specified and fundamentally broken. Their proponents haven't been addressing any of their concerns and tried to push a half baked version of them down everyone's throats, which was why they failed to pass. reply layer8 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n *sheer reply swiftcoder 4 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n This is fascinating to watch from the outside, because there was a big debate a few years ago about whether rust should go for ISO certification, and the consensus seemed to be it was a massive bureaucratic headache with few upsides. Not having a lot of insight into ISO procedures, I wasn't sure what to make of that, but this certainly gives some weight to that point of view reply skrrtww 9 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Personally I'd like to see C++ chill out on new features and maybe sort out the shitshow that is char8_t and std::filesystem. reply Krutonium 8 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Maybe a slightly faster std::regex or whatever it's called; apparently it's horrendously slow for no benefit. reply ameliaquining 8 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n The primary reason this hasn't happened is that it'd break C++'s de facto stable ABI (even though officially there's no such thing). For a lot more context, see https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2020/p20... and https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2019/p18... . Spoiler alert: The option described as \"the sort of leadership failing that I pray we can avoid\" is what ended up happening. reply imtringued 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I'm pretty sure that it is too late for C++ to break the \"stable ABI\" they already have. It's not too late to introduce an actual stable ABI though. However, it's not too late to do that in Rust either so I don't see the point in a language specific ABI in the future anyway. reply charcircuit 6 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Who cares about the ABI when the current ABI has a bunch of bad issues with it. For any backwards compatibility thing there is always AI that can rewrite old code. reply NekkoDroid 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n The compiler vendors care. It is also the reason why C++ never actually had a GC, even if the standard had it specified (and removed it with C++23). reply failbuffer 7 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n As an avid non-C++ programmer, can I suggest doing something about the build/module system? I shouldn't have to learn a separate language (CMake) and figure out the right include files to pull in a library. I mean, I mainly say this to joke because it's probably unfixable at this point, but whenever I mess with it, I'm struck by how hard/opaque C++ makes this problem compared to most other languages. reply jjmarr 6 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n There is no standard build/module system or way of dependency management. Unfortunately, this is because there's no standard compiler with standardized flags so C++ would also have to standardize compiler behaviour too. Large FAANGs that would be incentivized to change it don't care because they have their own in-house C++ build systems like bazel (Google) or buck2 (Meta) that are far faster than cmake (distributed compilation and caching) but require huge teams to maintain. Unfortunately, this distributed compilation and caching layer is something that many other languages don't have since their build systems are made for single devs building locally. Also the dependencies can be added in a myriad of ways. Your build system can download them, your meta-build dependency manager can find them (Conan), your Linux distro can provide them (system libraries), or maybe you just download an object file from a website + some headers and are told to figure it out yourself (many commercial packages like cuda or steam). reply weitendorf 2 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Like most things in C++, everybody who uses it professionally has already solved this in one way or another in a way that works for them. So this is only a painful problem for people who don't use C++ very much, and whatever centralized, official solution to builds would probably not really get adopted by most C++ shops. Because of that, even if it were solved, it's likely that it would only solve the problems that people who don't use C++ very much, or who don't invest in build tooling, want fixed. Which is basically what Make was originally. IMO it's best to think of C++ as a language only. Because of Microsoft/Sun/the web/etc and the desire to win over developers' business, a lot of newer languages are designed to be batteries-included with official first party tools that are supposed to be the canonical way to solve that particular class of problems. Those languages are essentially products or brands that are part of their benefactors' grand designs for specific business or career outcomes. C++ by contrast has multiple competing implementations for basically anything you can think of, and isn't someone's \"business\" the way most other languages are, so there's n