al attacks and a political issue. reply nukanse 8 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n C++ should focus into turning SIMD into a higher level language instead of being a giant collection of intrinsics. Avx code is unreadable. reply jandrewrogers 8 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n SIMD cannot be solved at the compiler level. The capabilities of silicon SIMD implementations are fundamentally so disjoint that code using SIMD requires contextual and platform-specific data structures and algorithms. SIMD on different silicon is incompatible at layers of abstraction far above the codegen manipulation a compiler can do. reply zamadatix 7 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n You'll never get the full feature set of all SIMD implementations in a single generic abstraction but I'm not sure that's the only way a SIMD abstraction could provide value. There is a pretty consistent set of narrow SIMD that behaves close enough to consistently get better performance across generic targets with high level abstractions better than auto vectorized scalar code but worse than platform tuned SIMD. reply jandrewrogers 7 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n The subset of SIMD that translates is so narrow that it is almost useless in real code. That’s the problem. Additionally, it is invalid for the compiler to redesign your data structures. The ability of compilers to use SIMD is no better than it was 20 years ago. Total stagnation for all practical purposes, and the reason is that compilers fundamentally lack the context and freedom to rewrite the code architecture appropriately for SIMD optimization (even if they could). I think a point people miss is that highly optimized SIMD code is almost never about parallelizing loops. It is about deeply probing and manipulating arbitrarily complex data structures in parallel that involve collections of unrelated types. SIMD allows you to do this, and it is fantastically efficient, but a compiler has no conceivable way of understanding this. For this reason, SIMD optimization has often been classified as an AI-Complete problem in computer science. reply throwaway17_17 6 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n My imagination is failing me, can you give me an example of the type of work being done where SIMD is ‘deeply probing and manipulating arbitrarily complex data structures in parallel that involve collections of unrelated types’? I don’t doubt you know what you mean, but I can’t wrap my head around it. For me I can only think of the simplest example of SIMD being used to ‘parallelize loops’ as what SIMD is for. If it turns out I’m missing a large problem or application space for SIMD I will be excited to research it. Any pointers to topics would be appreciated. reply jandrewrogers 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n SIMD is often used to evaluate complex data structures in parallel. A common case is given a row with an arbitrary mix of data types and a query with an arbitrary mix of independent constraints on those data types, to evaluate all of the constraints in parallel in the same SIMD instruction(s). The idea that SIMD is only good for parallelizing operations over arrays of the same type is empirically false and seriously understates the capabilities of good SIMD implementations. Some SQL-ish implementations work this way on row-structured data. Most devs seriously underestimate what good SIMD is capable of. reply throwaway17_17 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Thanks for the response, I’ll have some reading to do in this area. reply AlotOfReading 6 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n One example is OLAP databases, which often heavily exploit SIMD for query processing. You can also turn parsing into a SIMD problem, which is applicable to things like handling XML, JSON, and CSV. Simdjson comes to mind, or parabix for XML. reply throwaway17_17 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n The parsing stuff I am aware of, but the query processing is a big blank for me to dig into. reply smitherfield 6 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Couldn't SIMD features include marking data as \"the compiler is free to rearrange\"? reply gdwatson 7 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Is the common feature set described anywhere online? I am not in the SIMD world, and I am curious about the state of it. But I am not so committed that I am wiling to learn several different platforms’ SIMD implementations in order to suss out their commonalities. reply janwas 3 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Please stop repeating this ludicrously overstated claim. I have personally seen several hundred use cases for SIMD that are just fine with portable intrinsics, and NOT always \"platform-specific data structures and algorithms\". Sure, that does happen, but not fundamentally, nor even often. To be clear, I agree the compiler isn't solving the data layout problem for us, but portable intrinsics are indeed useful. gemma.cpp uses them, and last I checked, has much faster long-context prefill and decode vs. llama.cpp. They hand-roll the intrinsics and presumably it is harder to land optimizations repeated across 7+ implementations/instruction sets. reply wahern 6 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n > SIMD cannot be solved at the compiler level. ISPC doesn't a pretty good job of it: https://ispc.github.io/ reply bajard 7 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n That's why it needs a lot of work. Writing SIMD in c++ is like writing assembly. reply jfultz 6 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n In fact, C++26 made some considerable progress on that front. https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/experimental/simd An implementation of which exists in the following GitHub repo (albeit implemented in the xvec::simd namespace rather than std::simd...but otherwise intended to be spec-compliant). https://github.com/intel/xvec Speaking as a long-time C++ developer...I wasn't uninterested in contracts (which I've known about since the days of Bertrand Meyer flogging them in Eiffel), but I agree with the sentiment here that the SIMD efforts were considerably more interesting. reply janwas 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I wrote a comparison with Highway: https://github.com/google/highway/blob/master/g3doc/std_simd... std::simd is unfortunately very limited. reply nurettin 7 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n There are frameworks trying to unify simd/avx, but the abstractions aren't free. At this point, we need better hardware and low level interfaces, not better abstractions. reply zerr 2 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Can we have a 10 year break from further language changes? reply nxobject 1 hour ago | parent | next [–] \n Or, at the very, least a rule that a proposed feature should be a vendor-specific extension for at least 5 years, and the vendor has to dogfood it in their toolchain. reply Zepto8537 7 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n major C++26 features, including reflection, std::execution and library hardening, are also delayed because they were included in the same draft. reply dzogchen 5 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n No they are not. The standard may be delayed but most compilers ship these features already. reply fithisux 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Maybe it's time for C++ community to create maintenance releases of old standards? Not new features necessarily, but fixes or backports that fix major flaws. It can also complement old standards. If a C++23 feature can be supported in C++98 or C++11, it should go to that standard with an update. reply account42 2 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n That already exist - C++ defect reports can change the behavior of implementations for older standards. reply varjag 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n How would that even work? Implementations that’s been compliant for years suddenly become non-compliant? reply scotty79 6 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Honest question. If I want to spin up a new project with a coding agent to explore some idea and I'd like the bare metal performance, why would I choose C++ over Rust? I could choose TypeScript over Rust if I wanted it to be html first or I could choose Go if I wanted it to be clear of the complexity of memory management or I could choose C# if I wanted it to have best interface with windows stuff or I could choose something Erlang VM based for resilience and multithreading. But what reason might I have to pick C++? reply weitendorf 2 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Interfacing with other C++ projects or leveraging existing infrastructure/work you've done. Rust's package management and lack of stable, high-quality libraries maintained by professional or even just generally known entities are a pretty big problem. For the small number of Rust projects I've attempted, I've tried to make them zero-dependency, and it worked (you just ask agents to implement more libraries) but it added a lot of annoying overhead. It's also just easier to write high performance code in C/C++. If you'd rather have that than builtin memory safety guarantees or whatever else you want out of Rust, or have a small enough surface for errors/threats that you can just manually spend time validating that your code is safe and secure, C++ is still pretty good IMO. I would generally start with C though, because it makes interop with other languages easier reply creata 1 hour ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n If a coding agent is writing code for you, telling it what language to use is micromanaging. It might use Rust, it might use C++, it might use both. If it's writing the code, it probably has a better idea than you have for what's appropriate for that code. reply banjoisbanjo 6 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Maybe you don't. If you were to spin up a new project with a coding agent most of the subsequent highlights of the languages you cited might also not matter. reply jdw64 7 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n But I'm curious—why did DIS fail this time with C++26? Weren't feature additions in C++ usually plentiful? But they say this is the first case where a DIS failed during the standardization consensus process. Why is that? reply hankbond 9 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Maybe my reading comprehension has just nosedived but I could not find where this article discusses what the heck Contracts refers to? reply AlotOfReading 9 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n You'll find plenty of info if you search for anything like \"c++ contracts\" [0], but the short of it is that they're just pre/post conditions on functions. Slightly improved assertions, basically. [0] https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/language/contracts reply btown 8 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n https://www.cppstories.com/2026/cpp26_contracts/ is IMO a better introduction. cppreference is often a bit too technical for its own good :) But I think there's a broader issue which is that if it took me multiple searches to find a good-enough visual \"pitch\" for what contracts look like in practice, its champions have not yet won hearts and minds - which brings us to the situation at hand. reply bionhoward 8 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Oh im not a c++ guy but that actually looks pretty awesome because it’s like Hoare logic reply SolarNet 9 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Its a very inside baseball article. Contracts is a proposed C++ language feature for improving programmable assertions of program state. E.g. a better c++ version of C's assert function. That in theory will improve both safety and performance by letting people add more complex assertions (e.g. pre/post conditions) to functions. reply tonyhart7 2 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n why would any startup or company would care about C++ committee do ???? isn't this is hampering innovation ??? they can just simply use Zig or Rust no ?? reply mihaaly 3 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n > Now it gets complicated. It was said in the article about the process. But to me this is relevant to the state of the language itself. reply shevy-java 7 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n C++ is in a strange position. If you look at \"let's use AI to rewrite xyz in Rust\", then C++ has to wonder\nwhy it isn't as often mentioned compared to Rust here. I am not saying AI \nshould be the future, but merely compare why Rust is cited here more \nfrequently than C++. That's just one of many other things. Rust also got\npopular due to crates. How is the alternative called in C++? Barely anyone\noutside the C++ community knows. And that's also a problem. C++ isn't going away anytime soon, of course, but when people keep on mentioning\nalternatives again and again then it shows that the competition intensifed a lot. reply jeroenhd 5 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n I think Rust's saftey guarantees are an important reason to throw it at LLMs. LLMs make stupid mistakes, even the very best ones, and a lot of them, all the time. Having a compiler reject those mistakes makes for a better automated process. C++ has some safety mechanisms as well, but they're weak and fragile compared to Rust. For people with C++ experience working together months at a time that doesn't have to be a problem, but AI rewrites turn a million lines of one language into a million of the other in a day, followed by an impossibly short human review. Rust also isn't as safe and correct as you'd hope it to be for \"fuck it, let the LLM deal with it\" style programming, but it's got the best speed-usability-safety balance out of the common languages if you don't trust your programmers. reply thatxliner 6 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n > How is the alternative called in C++? what is it though? vcpkgs/decentralized repositories? reply smallstepforman 4 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n With package managers on Linux, BSD and Haiku, the only odd man out needing packages is Windows, and vcpkg fills this gap so its no longer an issue. reply filipdobro 6 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n It is de facto vcpkg reply gmueckl 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Hacker news has always been a rust bubble. Tallies of opinions voiced here are always skewed in some ways. reply scotty79 6 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Why would anyone want to rewrite anything in C++ given they can choose anything else? Like Rust or Go or C or Zig or literally anything else. reply nananana9 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I wouldn't choose Rust because of compile times. C++'s aren't great either, but with discipline in how you structure headers, you can get them down to a pretty reasonable place. Unsafe Rust is also a bit of a PITA, and I have cases where I care lot about precisely how memory is laid out and accessed, I'd have to resort to it quite a lot. I don't love crates either, they smell a bit like node_modules and all big Rust projects I've seen have hundreds of them. Most C++ libraries are self contained, by nature of not having an easy way to declare dependencies. I wouldn't choose Go because GC is a non-starter. I wouldn't choose C because it's nice to have some form of generics or metaprogramming, even if templates are not exactly fantastic. I wouldn't choose Zig because the language isn't stable, and the lead developer has hinted that they'll drop LLVM down the line, and I don't want to beta test their compiler backend. reply drnick1 4 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Because, used sensibly, C++ is a better C. This is more or less what C++98 was, and even that is probably too much. reply filipdobro 6 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Compiler stuff in MLIR. reply ranger_danger 9 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Maybe this time could be used to consider memory safety much more seriously. EDIT: Would love to know why people are so angry about that? reply SolarNet 9 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n The issue that caused this to be voted down is often seen as a memory safety solution (not something I personally 100% agree with, but thats a broad view). And the rest of this release includes other memory safety work. And it was voted down and delayed for apparent political reasons (again not a view I agree with). So most people will read your view as hypocritical/misinformed. reply MBCook 8 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n It could also be read as a straight up attack. Basically, a way of saying “why not follow Rust“ without having to say the word Rust. I think it was a fair question. As someone who doesn’t follow C++ I really don’t know what else was in the proposal. It’s nice to hear there is memory safety stuff. reply ameliaquining 6 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n The memory-safety-relevant parts of the proposal could be salvaged without keeping contracts more broadly, by saying that the relevant behaviors (like indexing past the end of a std::vector) cause program termination in hardened builds. I'm sure it's more complicated than this but I don't think much more. More generally, the C++ committee has had a really hard time agreeing on memory-safety stuff because there are a lot of different constituencies with completely different priorities (especially whether they're using it for greenfield vs. legacy vs. something in between), and the incentive is to make proposals sound good to everyone at the expense of actually being coherent. reply GalaxyNova 9 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Memory safety was solved in 1959 with the invention of lisp reply Surac 5 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I disapprove what they do to c++ for many years now, but nobody cares. I had hoped for c++ what has become c#. Well now i use c# with c if i need the speed reply vivzkestrel 9 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n - since you are a part of the voting group, i have a doubt - let us say tomorrow everyone were to consider removing all unsigned variables across c++ - what is the impact in terms of compatibility for all the old c++ applications which i believe would be millions of them if not billions? - the reason i ask is they say do not unsigned anything as much as you can because overflowing the bottom below 0 is easy - has there ever been any consideration to get rid of unsigned xyz in c++? reply vivzkestrel 7 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n you know downvoting without explaining why is part of herd mentality. i expected better on HN. dont you wanna give some reasons as to why you find this offensive? reply SP3269 9 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n Time to cancel the project. C++ acquired sufficient set of features over the years, becoming very PL/I-esque. reply drivebyhooting 8 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I used to be a C++ programmer but I haven’t done it in a few years. I pretty much do all my low level vibe coding in Rust. Is C++ still relevant? What prevents Rust from completely supplanting it? reply gucci-on-fleek 8 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Even if C++ is irrelevant for new code, there's so much software already written in it that we'll be maintaining it for decades to come. I'd argue that Cobol and Fortran have been mostly obsolete for new code for almost 30 years now, yet both are still widely used today, and they've even had major updates in the past decade [0] [1]. And there's even more software written in C++ than there is in Cobol or Fortran, so I don't think that C++ is going away any time soon. [0]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COBOL#COBOL_2023 [1]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortran#Fortran_2023 reply adgjlsfhk1 4 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n with ai their future seems very uncertain. ports are now very easy, so languages have to stand in their own merits reply gmueckl 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n That is a myoptic view. Old codebases often have their value measured in the tiny problems thay were ironed out over the decades and the knowledge that they cover their subject domain correctly. A complete rewrite loses all of this in an instant amd has to start from scratch. reply jandrewrogers 7 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Essentially all high-end systems code for high-performance and high-reliability systems is still written in modern C++. That language has many attractive properties, including safety features that Rust does not currently support. Additionally, for pure performance requirements, C++ is still materially faster than Rust in practice. That may change some day. C++ has many issues but it is also objectively excellent at many things. reply n_u 5 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > That language has many attractive properties, including safety features that Rust does not currently support. Could you expand on what specific safety features C++ has that Rust doesn't? reply fancyfredbot 8 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n C++ is the third most popular programming language according to TIOBE behind C and python. It's got an 8.87% ranking. So yes it looks like C++ is still relevant. In fact C++ usage has risen slightly. Rust is in tenth place with 1.44% ranking, it's rising fast but still has a long way to go before it catches C++. I'm not going to speculate about why Rust hasn't yet supplanted C++ but I will say I don't find it surprising. reply miffy900 7 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Please for God's sake stop citing TIOBE. A much more accurate snapshot would be Github - they recently published the top langauges for the year (so far, up til Sept 2026) by distinct monthly contributors and C++ comes in at 9 out of the top 10, and Rust doesn't even place in the top 10 yet, so yes it is highly relevant and prevalent, but not because TIOBE said so. But, TypeScript, Python and Rust are the fastest growing languages, in that order, so also you cannot discount Rust. https://github.blog/news-insights/octoverse/octoverse-commit... reply fancyfredbot 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n You didn't mention why people shouldn't cite TIOBE so I checked. It's because they measure search engine queries to assess popularity. GitHub measures actual code usage in real repos. I agree the GitHub methodology seems better. I don't think TIOBE methods mean that it's ranking is completely irrelevant but it'll be noisy and you could easily game it. reply gmueckl 3 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n Github is also skewed because it only captures a subset of open source projects, and open source is realistically a teeny tiny fraction of the world's written code. reply account42 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Yes, GitHub is heavily skewed towards \"hip\" projects. reply afdbcreid 8 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n TIOBE is nonsense. But, of course, there is still a lot of new C++ code written, more than Rust. I believe C++ will become legacy, but it's not there yet and even once that happens it'll take at least decades to stop writing C++. reply thw_9a83c 6 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n > Is C++ still relevant? What prevents Rust from completely supplanting it? A highly mature ecosystem with well-established libraries. Especially in certain fields (DSP, robotics, CAD kernels, microcontrollers, etc.), there are no equivalent libraries and frameworks in Rust with such a proven track record. Now, LLMs can rewrite those libraries in Rust quite easily. Such options weren't available a few years back. But it's still hard to prove that a rewrite hasn't introduced new bugs. It's easy to demonstrate that a piece of code has bugs, but much harder to prove that it doesn't introduce any new ones. reply willtemperley 2 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Additionally, even if an LLM-created Rust port of a C++ library was bug-free, an LLM cannot recreate the community that has evolved around the library. Take PROJ for example, which is the de-facto coordinate systems library in the geospatial world, mostly written in C++. The processes and trust built up with external organisations like national mapping agencies is irreplaceable. That hasn't stopped people trying to vibe-clone PROJ in Rust though. reply thw_9a83c 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n > ...an LLM cannot recreate the community that has evolved around the library. This is, in fact, an excellent point. You can ask an LLM to expertly clone or re-create any project in a different programming language. But that doesn't make you (or anyone else) an expert in that field, let alone competent in such a new codebase. Slowly developed, painfully debugged projects have a community of human brains who care about the topic. An LLM clone has nothing like that. reply anonymous908213 8 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n 99.9% of all system code in the world is still written in C/++. It runs your Java, C#, Javascript, Python, PHP, basically every production language that isn't Go or Rust. It runs the two most popular game engines in the world. It runs your phone, server, and laptop, no matter which of any you use. It runs your car. It runs almost every embedded and industrial application on the planet. Is it still relevant, JFC. The way people on this forum manage to sequester themselves from reality. Let me reverse the question: is Rust relevant? Mozilla developed Rust, abandoned their Rust browser, and only 10% of Firefox is written in Rust. Maybe 1% of modules in Chrome and Linux are written in Rust, if that. And what of Rust applications written from scratch? Can you name ten applications that have, say, exposure to more than 100,000 users? Impossible mode: they can't be developer tools only used by other developers (uv, ripgrep, etc.), they must actually be applications used by enterprises or consumers. reply devsda 8 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I read that as \"is c++ still relevant for new features, given Rust and its popularity\", not \"who is still using C++\". Personally, I think it is still relevant. Many people thought Kotlin could overtake Java, Java reinvented itself with new initiatives/features and was saved from becoming irrelevant. Rust also has the LLM advantage which is where I think the \"if it compiles it works\" nature of Rust is easier for manual changes and maintenance when needed. reply afdbcreid 8 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Java vs. Kotlin is a different matter. It's still an open question if C++ even could match Rust's features, not to say if it (the committee) wants to, and the answer seems to be \"no\". reply gmueckl 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n The feature parity question goes both ways: rust won't ever be able to express some things that are possible in C++. reply afdbcreid 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Like? reply qtqtqt 8 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n > basically every production language that isn't Go or Rust. Even in their case. Rust depends on LLVM which is wriiten in C++. Go needs libc on some platforms. reply adzm 8 hours ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n As a big c++ fan, new projects are generally choosing Rust, which I would agree with, even though I do indeed love C++ reply willtemperley 8 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Your Rust very likely runs in a C / C++ environment. reply printer_in_pink 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n rust depends on C++ (LLVM) reply voakbasda 9 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I feel like this is a bellwether event for society at large. Growing impasses between more and more segments of society. Diminishing trust leading to the inability to reach consensus. Maybe it’s just me though. reply SolarNet 9 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n Perhaps it is, in this case it would be between money and everyone else specifically. Contracts is a pet project of Microsoft and the heavy money parts of the committee. They've basically shouted down the dissenters, hence the effectively unprecedented action of the rubberstamp portion of the process saying they dont think consensus was reached in a kosher way. reply Maxatar 9 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n No one who developed contracts works for Microsoft. Of the like 20 people who contributed to the feature, 4 of them work for Bloomberg, so you could maybe argue that Bloomberg has a vested interest in the feature, but that's really stretching things. Support for contracts is fairly broad and spans numerous organizations. reply thefourthchime 7 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n I have no idea why anyone would work in C++ in 2026. If I had to work on an existing codebase, I'd just have Claude rewrite it in Rust. It's always been a miserable, awful mess. reply orphereus 4 hours ago | prev | next [–] \n How did C/C++ get into ISO? Why are they \"special\" compared to other languages?\nJust because they are old and ubiquitous in all systems programming? The whole design committee procedure around these languages feels like a bureaucratic nightmare, again compared to other languages, especially Rust. reply bobmarleybiceps 4 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n I think before I was born, there was just a ton of (closed-source) C compilers for different systems. So people were like \"we should have a standard, so our safety critical application doesn't break when we decide to use a <new hardware vendor's> C compiler.\" I also think since compiler infrastructure has improved so much, and everything is open source now ... so idk if it matters as much anymore. reply drnick1 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n > Why are they \"special\" compared to other languages? The world basically runs on C. reply jll29 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n There is no need for a language to be \"special\" just to get standardized. The main driver is a group of people willing to submit the necessary documents to ISO (or national bodies like ANSI or DIN or even IEEE, and then ISO may also adopt existing standards as theirs). COBOL, FORTRAN and Pascal are all ISO Standards [1,2,3]. [1] https://www.iso.org/standard/74527.html [2] https://www.iso.org/standard/82170.html [3] https://www.iso.org/standard/13802.html reply smallstepforman 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n It makes big companies and gov bodies happier since and code investment is guaranteed to run with competitors tool chains. You’re not locked in to one vendor, it is an international spec. reply ricardobeat 4 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Isn’t Rust also suffering from committee deadlock for the past couple years? reply Animats 8 hours ago | prev [–] \n At this point, changes to major programming languages have to adapt to a world in which AI systems generate and change much of the code. C++ contracts may be good or bad, but they are from before strong AI coding systems. So it's time to rethink. And we have no idea what AI systems will be doing in 2027. reply leecommamichael 7 hours ago | parent | next [–] \n > So it's time to rethink. > > And we have no idea what AI systems will be doing in 2027. How would you begin to form a plan of how you'll rethink if you can't even see into next year? Isn't it beneficial not to change the languages too much because there's so much training data for the current revisions? AI does not need to be catered to, that is the whole point and why it is useful. reply Animats 6 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n We're going to need languages which both humans and AIs can read and write.\nWe don't know what those look like yet. Contracts aren't a bad idea, but C++ has just become too big. Even people who used to be C++ language lawyers have said that. reply leecommamichael 6 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n Okay. reply ameliaquining 7 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Not to defend the particular proposal at issue here, but my sense is that contract-like things synergize quite well with AI coding, for the same reasons automated tests and static types do. reply 01100011 8 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n A lot of folks, many of them our most capable programmers, are fighting the last war. It’s like they think AI is going away and we’ll all work like it’s 2019 again. reply gmueckl 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n It looks like 2027 orb2028 will be the year when everyone gets so fed up with AI that those \"features\" will be forced to retreat from under everyone's nose. The stuff will not go away, but there is AI fatigue and it is slowly approaching a tipping point. reply wiseowise 4 hours ago | parent | prev [–] \n Wow. I’m surprised at the amount of downvotes. Are people really that in denial? reply \n \n Guidelines | FAQ | Lists | API | Security | Legal | Apply to YC | Contact \n Search: "
 },
