 "page:https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50369106": {
  "v": 4,
  "fetched_at": "2026-11-04T20:18:36.257Z",
  "bytes": 10250,
  "text": " A new Go API for JSON | Hacker News Hacker News new | past | comments | ask | show | jobs | submit login A new Go API for JSON ( go.dev ) 105 points by olexsmir 2 hours ago | hide | past | favorite | 29 comments help \n \n asim 37 minutes ago | next [–] \n At this stage to me Go is the new C compiler. Quite honestly I spent 10 years or more writing Go code. At times 18 hours a day. And now with AI, I get models to write my Go code. I STILL want it to be Go code. Is that strange? No because I understand the performance and safety of Go. But I rarely write it directly anymore. Probably not at all in 6-9 months. Yet I still occasionally look at the code to see what the output looks like. So yea hopefully a nice new addition but only my prompts now matter. So need to make sure they pickup on the new API. reply dofm 35 minutes ago | parent | next [–] \n This is mostly why I have come back to Go. I am using local LLMs and Go has a bunch of strengths where smaller contexts are concerned; less complicated flow of control being one of them. reply wiseowise 9 minutes ago | prev | next [–] \n This is how you roll out features, C++. Congrats to Go team and users. reply modulovalue 40 minutes ago | prev | next [–] \n I'm working on better SIMD support in Dart and one of the motivations for that work is specializing parser specifications based on schema specifications of that language. Or more concretely, if we had a parser generator + json parser specification that can generate efficient simdjson style parsers, then we should be able to parse json restricted to a subset of json based on a schema at least as efficiently as simdjson can parse general purpose json. That way we could have the best of both worlds, a (potentially lazy) statically type-safe model and simdjson performance! I still think that this is definitely doable, it's just that a lot of infrastructure needs to be built for it. I remember reading that Meta is doing something similar with binary formats. I find it weird that there's nothing like that for json yet. reply weitendorf 12 minutes ago | parent | next [–] \n I was working on metaparsing earlier this year (specifically over grpc because of its better support for types and better serialization format). I think most people who use JSON don't want strong typing or to use schemas. If you do, you just use a different serialization format: JSON is designed to be a generic, human-readable way to basically represent any kind of data structure (since it deserializes to JS objects). So everything about JSON is working against you if you do try to enforce type/schemas on it. A grpc-like protocol with more prescriptive type registries/discovery (over the network) and better interop with http (like grpc-gateway) would be the best approach IMO. Definitely not JSON schema reply lightandlight 28 minutes ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n This approach is called \"staged parsing\": https://dl.acm.org/doi/pdf/10.1145/2714064.2660241 https://dl.acm.org/doi/pdf/10.1145/3314221.3314625 reply CamouflagedKiwi 1 hour ago | prev | next [–] \n I generally like `encoding/json/v2` and I'm glad they did it. I wish they'd left `MarshalIndent` in there though. The equivalent code is super complicated and non-obvious, so it's a pain to migrate, and it's a pain when you're banging out some quick code and want to dump some readable JSON. I suppose these days the LLMs can write the complicated version for us, of course. reply athorax 1 hour ago | parent | next [–] \n I'm not sure if I'm missing something, but isn't this functionality just moved into jsontext? https://pkg.go.dev/encoding/json/jsontext#Value.Indent v := jsontext.Value(b)\n v.Indent()\n fmt.Println(string(v)) reply arccy 54 minutes ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n json.Marshal(v, jsontext.WithIndent(\" \"))\n\n? reply cbarrick 25 minutes ago | prev | next [–] \n The fact that this took 6 years to land stood out to me. I'm not entirely surprised, and I'm glad the Go team is carefully considering their interfaces before launching. But 6 years seems like a lot of work to land a JSON API. reply awesome_dude 13 minutes ago | parent | next [–] \n Sometimes I wonder about Go's velocity - generics (something that I still haven't used beyond the tutorials) took an age as well JSON processing, quite markedly, is renown in Go for being slow, so I don't really understand why it took so long reply hnlmorg 3 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I think in the case of JSON, it was “good enough”. People who found this to be a problem would use one of the many 3rd party options. I kind of like that the standard library doesn’t move fast. It makes it easier to fit the ecosystem into my head (unlike, for example, the JavaScript ecosystem where I find code doesn’t survive 2 years before failing to compile). But I do agree that slow and steady produces its own problems. Your example of generics is a good one. But think the example of Go modules is another, perhaps even better, example of how sometimes the Go team really should have accelerated their efforts on. reply dofm 36 minutes ago | prev | next [–] \n I… think this is good? I'm just getting the neuron-band back together where Golang is concerned. I have done some in the past for little system tasks, but I am switching away from interpreted languages entirely for web stuff, and initially to Go, as I switch away from hand-coding to at least LLM-supported coding. So I am a bit out of date on the pain points (I never really felt pain points when I last used it, because the jobs I was doing had very small, quite Go-friendly shapes). reply troupo 15 minutes ago | prev | next [–] \n > in any, out any Completely unrelated: can anyone tell me why language authors introduce in/out/inout function parameters (see also C#). It feels like this is some rather bad workaround for something that breaks assumptions about parameters being passed into the function. reply lolpanda 1 hour ago | prev [–] \n the marshal api has been very annoying. why does marshal return an error? the only reason the marshal can return an error is that the input type is not marshalable. it's certainly a code bug. to me it's like int+str returning an error instead of compiler error reply duskwuff 33 minutes ago | parent | next [–] \n > why does marshal return an error? the only reason the marshal can return an error is that the input type is not marshalable Not necessarily. Among other things, MarshalJSON methods called during marshaling can return an error for whatever reason the developer sees fit - one possibility that comes to mind is that part of the object tree being marshalled is lazily loaded, and failed to do so. And, as others have mentioned, there are types like \"any\" whose marshalability can't be determined at compile time. Those types aren't necessarily at the top level, either; they could appear anywhere in the object tree. reply sgsjchs 41 minutes ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Types may be mostly marshallabble, have a subset of values that are unmarshallable. E.g. non-finite floats. reply hnlmorg 1 hour ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n I wouldn’t class unmarshallable data as a compile time error. Plus the point of the marshal interface is that it’s reusable. reply thayne 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] \n It could be if go had a more powerful type system. reply hnlmorg 58 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n This isn’t a type system problem. It’s a data origin problem. Ie it could be any random JSON blob a user entered. The compiler doesn’t know this ahead of time. If you wanted type safety with JSON in Go then don’t use the marshal interface. Period. reply thayne 25 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n For unmarshalling, but the original complaint was about marshalling. And for that the type system could help if the marshal API only allowed you to marshal objects of types that supported marshalling. reply hnlmorg 12 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n I’d already addressed that when I said the marshal API is an interface that’s meant to be reusable across generalise use cases. it can be used for a variety of data formats and not just JSON. Go has this concept baked into the language. For example read and write interfaces so you can write data to an endpoint without having to worry about whether it’s a file system object, TCP/UDP socket, gzip achieve, and a plethora of other options too. It’s a nice convenience feature and makes up considerably for the restrictions that the Go type system places. And if you need higher performance code, or compile time safety, or have any other constraints that limit your ability to use these generalised interfaces, then you can use different APIs from the Go standard library to achieve the same thing. You don’t need to call json.Marshal() if you don’t want to. It’s an option, but it’s just one API into the JSON toolbox. reply 0x696C6961 38 minutes ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n Then write yourself a MustMarshal wrapper that panics. reply hnlmorg 2 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] \n That doesn’t magically make the compiler spot type system problems at compile time. Which is the point the GP was making. reply jeremyloy_wt 36 minutes ago | root | parent | prev | next [–] \n A generic Must[T] you mean ;) reply leetrout 1 hour ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n JSON is a runtime input of unknown type but in your example those are known types so the compiler can catch them. reply lolpanda 39 minutes ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n i meant i hoped it's panic as opposed to return an error. if there was an error, how am i supposed to handle it? can i tell the user hey sorry my bad it's likely a code error? crash is better than possible eating the error siliently reply NoDodgeQuestion 1 hour ago | parent | prev | next [–] \n do not know at compile time that input is not marshalable, every language marshal must return error for this reason, not only golang reply iaaan 53 minutes ago | parent | prev [–] \n It is trivial to ignore an error in Go if you know your use case cannot result in one. But at the end of the day, json.Marshal takes an `any` as a parameter, so it must return an error. reply \n \n Guidelines | FAQ | Lists | API | Security | Legal | Apply to YC | Contact \n Search: "
 },
 "page:https://www.welivesecurity.com/en/eset-research/slippynunchucks-hijacks-sogou-input-methods-brand-promotion-mechanism-to-distribute-malware/": {
