focus requirements and executable verifiers. The switched session interleaves ordered stages from other unfinished tasks in the same repository, while only the final focus patch is scored. \\ourmethod~contains 200 focus tasks from 19 Python repositories, reconstructed from issue, pull-request, and review histories and rendered as 400 matched session specifications. Across 7 coding agents , interleaved unfinished work produces an average focus-task resolution gap of 11.5 percentage points, ranging from 6.0 to 15.0 percentage points. Trajectory analysis identifies cross-task requirement confusion and loss of task state as recurrent resumption behaviors. We introduce a plug-in context-management module that selects task-relevant history and maintains concise task-state summaries . On OpenCode, the module improves focus-task completion under same-repository task switching by 5.5 to 15.5 percentage points across the evaluated models. Code is available at github.com/Miraclecsc/Switch-SWE. View arXiv page View PDF Project page GitHub 26 Add to collection Community Mqleet Paper author Paper submitter about 14 hours ago Switch-SWE is a repository-level benchmark for evaluating whether coding agents can resume a focus task after working on other unfinished tasks in the same repository. Each task is evaluated in matched uninterrupted and switched sessions, requiring agents to recover task-specific requirements and partial progress while avoiding cross-task confusion. \n The benchmark provides 200 focus tasks from 19 Python repositories, 400 matched session specifications, and executable verifiers. Across seven coding agents, task switching reduces focus-task resolution by 11.5 percentage points on average. A plug-in context-management module that selects relevant history and maintains task-state summaries improves completion by 5.5–15.5 percentage points on OpenCode. \n 🌐 Paper: https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.02017 🔥 Project Page: https://mqleet.github.io/Switch-Swe/ 🕹️ Code: http